var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"title":"Loci and recombination","page":"Loci and recombination","location":"tutorials/07_loci_and_recombination/#Loci-and-recombination","category":"section","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nSetup\nWhat gametes look like\nWhy it matters for the results\nWhat more loci cost"},{"title":"Overview","page":"Loci and recombination","location":"tutorials/07_loci_and_recombination/#Overview","category":"section","text":"The paper’s populations carry 100, 500, 1000 or 5000 loci and recombine them with a mean of three or five crossovers per gamete (Pagel et al. 2025). This vignette looks at how gametes are made, at a feature of QTSim’s recombination that the published results depend on, and at what more loci cost.\n\nEach gamete is built from one parent’s two chromosomes. The number of crossover points is drawn from a Poisson distribution with mean RecombPoissonMean (none if the draw is zero or at least the number of loci), the points are placed at distinct random positions, and the gamete starts on a randomly chosen parental chromosome and switches strands at the crossover points. In QTSim the copying loop never advances to the next point, so only the lowest point ever switches: every gamete has at most one crossover, however many points were drawn. The published simulations were run that way. The package reproduces it by default (CrossoverModel = :qtsim) and offers the evidently intended model, in which every point switches, as CrossoverModel = :all."},{"title":"Setup","page":"Loci and recombination","location":"tutorials/07_loci_and_recombination/#Setup","category":"section","text":"using QTLSim\nusing CairoMakie, Printf, Random, Statistics\nCairoMakie.activate!(type = \"png\")"},{"title":"What gametes look like","page":"Loci and recombination","location":"tutorials/07_loci_and_recombination/#What-gametes-look-like","category":"section","text":"gamete! builds one gamete from two parental chromosomes. With one parental chromosome all zeros and the other all ones, the gamete shows which parent each locus came from. Here are 40 gametes from 100 loci with a mean of five crossover points, under each model.\n\nL = 100\nA = zeros(L); B = ones(L); dest = zeros(L)\nrng = Xoshiro(1)\ndraw(allpoints) = reduce(hcat, [copy(gamete!(dest, A, B, rng, 5.0, L, allpoints)) for _ in 1:40])\nqtsim, every = draw(false), draw(true)\nswitches(G) = [count(i -> G[i, j] != G[i+1, j], 1:L-1) for j in axes(G, 2)]\n@printf(\"mean crossovers per gamete: QTSim model %.2f, every point %.2f\\n\",\n        mean(switches(qtsim)), mean(switches(every)))\n\nmean crossovers per gamete: QTSim model 1.00, every point 5.30\n\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 330))\nfor (k, (G, name)) in enumerate(((qtsim, \"QTSim (:qtsim)\"), (every, \"every point (:all)\")))\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, k], title = name, xlabel = \"locus\", ylabel = k == 1 ? \"gamete\" : \"\")\n    heatmap!(ax, 1:L, 1:40, G, colormap = [:lightblue, :navy])\nend\nfig\n\n<div id=\"fig-gametes\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 1: Forty gametes (rows) from a parent with one chromosome of zeros (light) and one of ones (dark), 100 loci, a mean of five crossover points. Under QTSim’s model (left) each gamete switches strands at most once; when every point switches (right) gametes are mosaics.\n\n</div>\n\nWhatever the crossover pattern, a gamete takes each locus from one parental chromosome, and the starting chromosome is chosen fairly. On average, then, a gamete carries half of the parent’s additive genotype under either model, so neither biases the mean. Lean proves this for any assignment of loci to strands (gamete_expected_sum_by in lean/CategoricalQuantGen/Recombination.lean). With random allelic effects:\n\na = randn(Xoshiro(2), L); b = randn(Xoshiro(3), L)\nfor allpoints in (false, true)\n    s = [sum(gamete!(dest, a, b, rng, 3.0, L, allpoints)) for _ in 1:100_000]\n    @printf(\"%-6s mean gamete genotype %.3f ± %.3f (standard error)   half the parent's genotype %.3f\\n\",\n            allpoints ? \":all\" : \":qtsim\", mean(s), std(s) / sqrt(length(s)), (sum(a) + sum(b)) / 2)\nend\n\n:qtsim mean gamete genotype -1.013 ± 0.025 (standard error)   half the parent's genotype -1.047\n:all   mean gamete genotype -1.066 ± 0.024 (standard error)   half the parent's genotype -1.047"},{"title":"Why it matters for the results","page":"Loci and recombination","location":"tutorials/07_loci_and_recombination/#Why-it-matters-for-the-results","category":"section","text":"Recombination does not change the mean, but it changes how genetic variance is distributed among gametes, and so how much variance the population holds and how fast it can respond to selection. To see the effect, the same populations are run under both models: 100 loci with five crossover points per gamete, paper-like parameters, 10,000 generations of stabilising selection and then a continuously moving optimum, for 48 seeds each.\n\npaperlike = Params(PopSize = 1000, Ploidy = 2, NoQTL = 100, NoGenerations = 12_000,\n                   PhenotypeVar = 0.022, Heritability = 0.32, MutationScalar = 0.004,\n                   MutationRatePerGamete = 1e-4, RecombPoissonMean = 5.0, InitFitnessSD = 5.0,\n                   MoveFitnessSDGen = 10_000, MoveFitnessSD = 0.05, MoveType = :continuous)\njobs = [(model, seed) for model in (:qtsim, :all) for seed in 1:48]\nres = Vector{Any}(undef, length(jobs))\nThreads.@threads for k in eachindex(jobs)\n    model, seed = jobs[k]\n    o = simulate(Params(paperlike; CrossoverModel = model); seed)\n    j = findfirst(==(9_999), o.gen)\n    res[k] = (; model, VG = o.geno_var[j], T = o.extinct ? o.extinct_gen - 9_999 : 2_000)\nend\n\nsumm(model, f) = [log(f(r)) for r in res if r.model === model]\nfor (name, f) in ((\"genetic variance before the move\", r -> r.VG),\n                  (\"generations to extinction\", r -> r.T))\n    q, a = summ(:qtsim, f), summ(:all, f)\n    δ = mean(a) - mean(q); se = sqrt(var(a) / length(a) + var(q) / length(q))\n    @printf(\"%-33s every-point / QTSim model: ×%.2f  (95%% interval ×%.2f–×%.2f)\\n\",\n            name, exp(δ), exp(δ - 1.96se), exp(δ + 1.96se))\nend\n\ngenetic variance before the move  every-point / QTSim model: ×1.08  (95% interval ×0.99–×1.17)\ngenerations to extinction         every-point / QTSim model: ×1.10  (95% interval ×1.08–×1.12)\n\nApplying every crossover point lets the populations survive the moving optimum about 10% longer, and they tend to hold more genetic variance (in these runs the interval for the variance only just includes no difference). The comparison of the Julia model with the paper’s published results (Validation against QTSim) shows the same effect across all the paper’s strata, and shows that only QTSim’s model reproduces the published numbers."},{"title":"What more loci cost","page":"Loci and recombination","location":"tutorials/07_loci_and_recombination/#What-more-loci-cost","category":"section","text":"Most of the work in a generation is copying chromosomes into gametes and summing genotypes, so the time per generation grows with the number of loci.\n\nLs = [1, 10, 100, 500, 1000, 5000]\ncosts = map(Ls) do L\n    q = Params(paperlike; NoQTL = L, NoGenerations = 40, MoveFitnessSDGen = -1)\n    simulate(Params(q; NoGenerations = 2); seed = 1)              # compile\n    1000 * minimum(@elapsed(simulate(q; seed = s)) for s in 1:3) / q.NoGenerations\nend\nfor (L, c) in zip(Ls, costs); @printf(\"%5d loci: %7.3f ms per generation\\n\", L, c); end\n\n    1 loci:   0.130 ms per generation\n   10 loci:   0.355 ms per generation\n  100 loci:   0.454 ms per generation\n  500 loci:   0.992 ms per generation\n 1000 loci:   1.798 ms per generation\n 5000 loci:   6.533 ms per generation\n\nfig = Figure(size = (480, 360))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xscale = log10, yscale = log10, xlabel = \"number of loci\",\n          ylabel = \"milliseconds per generation\")\nscatterlines!(ax, Ls, costs, color = :navy)\nfig\n\n<div id=\"fig-cost\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 2: Time per generation against the number of loci for 1000 diploid individuals, on one thread. With a few loci the fixed cost of each individual dominates; from a few hundred loci on, the cost grows steadily with the number of loci.\n\n</div>\n\nA 5000-locus population takes several milliseconds per generation, roughly fifteen times as long as a 100-locus one, so the paper’s 250,000-generation runs at that size are expensive; the replication therefore samples the paper’s inputs and stops each run 2000 generations after the optimum starts to move.\n\n<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-Pagel2025\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nPagel, Mark, Jacob D. Gardner, and Andrew Meade. 2025. “Quantitative-Genetic Analysis of Directional Adaptation Suggests Low Maximum Sustainable Rates of Change in Agreement with Data from Field Populations.” Scientific Reports 15. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-24445-2.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"title":"Mutation–selection balance","page":"Mutation–selection balance","location":"tutorials/02_mutation_selection_balance/#Mutation–selection-balance","category":"section","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nSetup\nThe mutational input\nWhere the “analytical” fitness width comes from\nEquilibrium across the paper’s parameter space\nMore loci, more variance"},{"title":"Overview","page":"Mutation–selection balance","location":"tutorials/02_mutation_selection_balance/#Overview","category":"section","text":"Before the optimum moves, each population in the paper spends 10,000 generations under stabilising selection, so that it starts the experiment at equilibrium. How much genetic variance it holds then matters: the genetic variance V_G is the raw material of the response to a moving optimum. This vignette measures the equilibrium and compares it with two classical approximations.\n\nMutation adds variance and stabilising selection and drift remove it. Write V_m for the variance mutation adds per generation, V_s = omega^2 + V_E for the width of selection on genotypes (the fitness function, widened by environmental noise), n for the number of loci, mu for the mutation rate per locus, alpha^2 for the variance of a mutation’s effect and N_e for the effective population size.\n\nLande’s Gaussian approximation (Lande 1975) assumes many alleles of small effect: V_G approx sqrtV_m V_s.\nThe stochastic house-of-cards approximation (Bürger et al. 1989) allows for rare mutations of large effect and for drift: V_G approx 4 n mu V_s  left(1 + V_s  (N_e alpha^2)right). This is the approximation the paper compares its simulations with (its Supplementary Figure S1, labelled equation 21a in its results table)."},{"title":"Setup","page":"Mutation–selection balance","location":"tutorials/02_mutation_selection_balance/#Setup","category":"section","text":"using QTLSim\nusing CairoMakie, Distributions, Printf, Random, Statistics\nCairoMakie.activate!(type = \"png\")\nThreads.nthreads()\n\n6"},{"title":"The mutational input","page":"Mutation–selection balance","location":"tutorials/02_mutation_selection_balance/#The-mutational-input","category":"section","text":"Each offspring chromosome receives a Poisson number of mutations with mean nmu, and each mutation adds a Gaussian effect of variance alpha^2 = m V_E  (2 n mu) to a random locus. The scaling is chosen so that, in expectation, mutation adds exactly V_m = m V_E to the genetic variance of a diploid individual each generation, whatever the number of loci and the mutation rate. That statement is proved in Lean (mutationalVarianceInput_eq in lean/CategoricalQuantGen/Mutation.lean); here we check it by applying the mutation operator to a large population of zero genomes, for several locus counts and rates.\n\nfunction mutational_input(p; rounds = 20, seed = 1)\n    w = World(p); rng = Xoshiro(seed); Δ = Float64[]\n    for _ in 1:rounds\n        fill!(w.sgenome, 0.0)\n        mutate!(w, rng)                       # adds mutations to the offspring buffer\n        append!(Δ, sum(@view w.sgenome[:, :, j]) for j in 1:p.PopSize)\n    end\n    var(Δ)\nend\n\nfor (k, (L, rate)) in enumerate(((10, 1e-3), (100, 1e-4), (100, 1e-2), (1000, 1e-3)))\n    p = Params(PopSize = 20_000, Ploidy = 2, NoQTL = L, MutationRatePerGamete = rate,\n               PhenotypeVar = 0.02, Heritability = 0.4, MutationScalar = 0.004)\n    @printf(\"NoQTL = %4d, rate = %.0e (%4.2f mutations per gamete):  variance added %.3e   (m·V_E = %.3e)\\n\",\n            L, rate, L * rate, mutational_input(p; seed = k), muteVar(p))\nend\n\nNoQTL =   10, rate = 1e-03 (0.01 mutations per gamete):  variance added 4.901e-05   (m·V_E = 4.800e-05)\nNoQTL =  100, rate = 1e-04 (0.01 mutations per gamete):  variance added 4.770e-05   (m·V_E = 4.800e-05)\nNoQTL =  100, rate = 1e-02 (1.00 mutations per gamete):  variance added 4.778e-05   (m·V_E = 4.800e-05)\nNoQTL = 1000, rate = 1e-03 (1.00 mutations per gamete):  variance added 4.810e-05   (m·V_E = 4.800e-05)\n\nThe input agrees with m V_E to within sampling error in every case, even though the number of mutations per generation differs a hundred-fold between the rows."},{"title":"Where the “analytical” fitness width comes from","page":"Mutation–selection balance","location":"tutorials/02_mutation_selection_balance/#Where-the-“analytical”-fitness-width-comes-from","category":"section","text":"When InitFitnessSD is not given, QTSim and this package use omega = V_G  sqrtm V_E with V_G = h^2 V_P. That is the Gaussian approximation solved for omega, with V_s approx omega^2: if V_G = sqrtV_m omega^2 and V_m = m V_E, then omega = V_G  sqrtm V_E. The algebra is proved in lean/CategoricalQuantGen/Heritability.lean (analyticalFitSD_eq). The width is therefore exactly the one that would hold the population at the target h^2 V_P if the Gaussian approximation were right, which makes it a direct test of that approximation. The paper itself does not use it: it draws omega at random for each population."},{"title":"Equilibrium across the paper’s parameter space","page":"Mutation–selection balance","location":"tutorials/02_mutation_selection_balance/#Equilibrium-across-the-paper’s-parameter-space","category":"section","text":"We draw 24 parameter sets from the paper’s priors (Pagel et al. 2025, Table 1): phenotypic variance V_P exponential with mean 0.018 above 0.01 and at most 0.09, heritability Weibull with shape 1.6 and scale 0.4, mutation scalar gamma with shape 1.1 and scale 0.004, and fitness width uniform on (0 10). Each population has 1000 individuals and 100 loci with mutation rate 10^-4, as in the paper, and runs for 10,000 generations; we average the genetic variance and the number of survivors over the last 1000.\n\nfunction draw_params(rng)\n    VP = 0.01 + rand(rng, Exponential(0.018))\n    while VP > 0.09; VP = 0.01 + rand(rng, Exponential(0.018)); end\n    h2 = rand(rng, Weibull(1.6, 0.4))\n    while !(0.01 <= h2 <= 0.99); h2 = rand(rng, Weibull(1.6, 0.4)); end\n    (; VP, h2, m = rand(rng, Gamma(1.1, 0.004)), ω = rand(rng, Uniform(0, 10)))\nend\n\nfunction equilibrium(θ; L = 100, gens = 10_000, seed)\n    p = Params(PopSize = 1000, Ploidy = 2, NoQTL = L, NoGenerations = gens,\n               PhenotypeVar = θ.VP, Heritability = θ.h2, MutationScalar = θ.m,\n               MutationRatePerGamete = 1e-4, RecombPoissonMean = 3.0, InitFitnessSD = θ.ω)\n    o = simulate(p; seed)\n    o.extinct && return nothing\n    last = o.gen .>= gens - 1000\n    (; p, VG = mean(o.geno_var[last]), ns = mean(o.nsurv[last]))\nend\n\ndraws = [draw_params(Xoshiro(k)) for k in 1:24]\neq = Vector{Any}(undef, length(draws))\nThreads.@threads for k in eachindex(draws)\n    eq[k] = equilibrium(draws[k]; seed = k)\nend\neq = filter(!isnothing, eq)\nlength(eq)\n\n24\n\nThe two approximations, written for a Params value. For N_e we use the number of survivors: each of the 2N gametes of the next generation comes from a survivor chosen at random, so the number a survivor contributes is binomial with variance V_k = 2N (n_s - 1)n_s^2 when n_s individuals survive, and N_e = 4N(V_k + 2) (Falconer and Mackay 1996). This is how the paper’s results table computes it.\n\ngaussian(p) = sqrt(muteVar(p) * (initFitSD(p)^2 + envVar(p)))\n\nfunction house_of_cards(p, ns)\n    N, n, μ = p.PopSize, p.NoQTL, p.MutationRatePerGamete\n    Vs = initFitSD(p)^2 + envVar(p)\n    α2 = muteVar(p) / (2n * μ)            # variance of one mutation's effect\n    Vk = 2N * (ns - 1) / ns^2              # variance in gametes contributed per survivor\n    Ne = 4N / (Vk + 2)\n    4n * μ * Vs / (1 + Vs / (Ne * α2))\nend\n\nshc = [house_of_cards(r.p, r.ns) for r in eq]\ngau = [gaussian(r.p) for r in eq]\nsim = [r.VG for r in eq]\n@printf(\"simulated / house of cards: median %.2f (range %.2f–%.2f)\\n\",\n        median(sim ./ shc), extrema(sim ./ shc)...)\n@printf(\"simulated / Gaussian:       median %.2f (range %.2f–%.2f)\\n\",\n        median(sim ./ gau), extrema(sim ./ gau)...)\n\nsimulated / house of cards: median 0.95 (range 0.69–1.50)\nsimulated / Gaussian:       median 1.28 (range 0.19–4.57)\n\nfig = Figure(size = (820, 380))\nfor (k, (pred, name)) in enumerate(((shc, \"stochastic house of cards\"), (gau, \"Gaussian (Lande)\")))\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, k], xscale = log10, yscale = log10, title = name,\n              xlabel = \"predicted V_G\", ylabel = \"simulated V_G\")\n    lo, hi = extrema(vcat(pred, sim))\n    lines!(ax, [lo / 2, 2hi], [lo / 2, 2hi], color = :gray, linestyle = :dash)\n    scatter!(ax, pred, sim, color = :teal)\nend\nfig\n\n<div id=\"fig-balance\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 1: Equilibrium genetic variance of 24 simulated populations against the two approximations (the line is equality). The stochastic house of cards tracks the simulations across nearly three orders of magnitude; the Gaussian approximation scatters widely, missing by up to a factor of about five in either direction.\n\n</div>\n\nThe house-of-cards regime is the relevant one here because mutation is rare: with 100 loci and a rate of 10^-4 there is only about one new mutation per hundred gametes, each with an effect large compared with the standing variation at its locus. One consequence is that the heritability in Params is not the heritability the population ends up with. It only sets the environmental variance, V_E = (1-h^2)V_P; the genetic variance is whatever mutation, selection and drift balance at.\n\nfig = Figure(size = (420, 380))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"nominal h²\", ylabel = \"realised h²\", limits = (0, 1, 0, 1))\nlines!(ax, [0, 1], [0, 1], color = :gray, linestyle = :dash)\nscatter!(ax, [r.p.Heritability for r in eq],\n         [r.VG / (r.VG + envVar(r.p)) for r in eq], color = :crimson)\nfig\n\n<div id=\"fig-heritability\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 2: Realised heritability VG/(VG + V_E) at equilibrium against the nominal heritability that set the environmental variance.\n\n</div>"},{"title":"More loci, more variance","page":"Mutation–selection balance","location":"tutorials/02_mutation_selection_balance/#More-loci,-more-variance","category":"section","text":"The paper simulated 100, 500, 1000 and 5000 loci. With the mutational input held fixed, more loci means more frequent mutations of smaller effect, which selection removes less efficiently, so the house of cards predicts more variance; the Gaussian approximation does not depend on the number of loci at all. Here one parameter set is run with 10 to 1000 loci.\n\nθ = (VP = 0.02, h2 = 0.4, m = 0.004, ω = 2.0)\nLs = [10, 30, 100, 300, 1000]\nbyL = Vector{Any}(undef, length(Ls))\nThreads.@threads for k in eachindex(Ls)\n    byL[k] = equilibrium(θ; L = Ls[k], seed = 100 + k)\nend\nfor (L, r) in zip(Ls, byL)\n    @printf(\"NoQTL = %4d:  simulated %.4f   house of cards %.4f   Gaussian %.4f\\n\",\n            L, r.VG, house_of_cards(r.p, r.ns), gaussian(r.p))\nend\n\nNoQTL =   10:  simulated 0.0106   house of cards 0.0137   Gaussian 0.0139\nNoQTL =   30:  simulated 0.0365   house of cards 0.0320   Gaussian 0.0139\nNoQTL =  100:  simulated 0.0465   house of cards 0.0599   Gaussian 0.0139\nNoQTL =  300:  simulated 0.0626   house of cards 0.0797   Gaussian 0.0139\nNoQTL = 1000:  simulated 0.0694   house of cards 0.0901   Gaussian 0.0139\n\nfig = Figure(size = (520, 380))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xscale = log10, xlabel = \"number of loci\", ylabel = \"equilibrium V_G\")\nlines!(ax, Ls, [house_of_cards(r.p, r.ns) for r in byL], color = :teal, label = \"house of cards\")\nhlines!(ax, [gaussian(byL[1].p)], color = :gray, linestyle = :dash, label = \"Gaussian\")\nscatter!(ax, Ls, [r.VG for r in byL], color = :crimson, label = \"simulated\")\naxislegend(ax, position = :lt)\nfig\n\n<div id=\"fig-loci\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 3: Equilibrium genetic variance against the number of loci for one parameter set (points), with the house-of-cards prediction (solid) and the Gaussian prediction, which does not depend on the number of loci (dashed).\n\n</div>\n\nThe equilibrium variance grows with the number of loci, as the house of cards predicts, while the Gaussian approximation misses the trend entirely. In these single runs the house of cards overestimates the level by about a quarter at 100 loci and above. The replication vignette makes the same comparison for the paper’s own parameter sets.\n\n<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-Burger1989\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nBürger, Reinhard, Günter P. Wagner, and Franz Stettinger. 1989. “How Much Heritable Variation Can Be Maintained in Finite Populations by Mutation-Selection Balance?” Evolution 43 (8): 1748–66. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1558-5646.1989.tb02624.x.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-FalconerMackay1996\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nFalconer, Douglas S., and Trudy F. C. Mackay. 1996. Introduction to Quantitative Genetics. 4th ed. Longman.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Lande1975\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nLande, Russell. 1975. “The Maintenance of Genetic Variability by Mutation in a Polygenic Character with Linked Loci.” Genetical Research 26 (3): 221–35. https://doi.org/10.1017/S0016672300016037.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Pagel2025\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nPagel, Mark, Jacob D. Gardner, and Andrew Meade. 2025. “Quantitative-Genetic Analysis of Directional Adaptation Suggests Low Maximum Sustainable Rates of Change in Agreement with Data from Field Populations.” Scientific Reports 15. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-24445-2.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"title":"API reference","page":"API reference","location":"api/#API-reference","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Simulation","page":"API reference","location":"api/#Simulation","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Derived quantities","page":"API reference","location":"api/#Derived-quantities","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"The categorical layer","page":"API reference","location":"api/#The-categorical-layer","category":"section","text":"These functions are implemented by the package extension QTLSimCatlabExt, which Julia loads when Catlab is loaded:\n\nusing QTLSim\nimport Catlab\nusing Catlab: ⋅, ⊗\nL = lifecycle()                                                        # the life cycle as syntax\n(; develop, select, reproduce, mutate, swap, generation) = operators()  # its image in the kernel"},{"title":"The life cycle and its lowerings","page":"API reference","location":"api/#The-life-cycle-and-its-lowerings","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Operators","page":"API reference","location":"api/#Operators","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"The population as an ACSet","page":"API reference","location":"api/#The-population-as-an-ACSet","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Types of the extension","page":"API reference","location":"api/#Types-of-the-extension","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"QTLSim.QTLSim","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.QTLSim","category":"module","text":"QTLSim\n\nA Julia port of QTSim, the quantitative-genetics simulation of Pagel, Gardner and Meade (Scientific Reports, 2025; the C source calls itself QTLSim 1.0): an additive polygenic trait under Gaussian stabilising viability selection toward an optimum that can move, with random mating, Poisson recombination and Poisson–Gaussian mutation. The kernel reproduces QTSim's numerics; validate/ compares it with QTSim and with the paper's published results.\n\nThe categorical layer, in which the population is an ACSet and the life cycle is a composite of operators (⋅) that can be stratified into demes (⊗), is the package extension QTLSimCatlabExt. Julia loads it when Catlab is loaded (import Catlab). Its functions are declared, documented and exported here.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.Params","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.Params","category":"type","text":"Params\n\nConfiguration for one simulation. Heritability is the narrow-sense heritability h² (a single scalar); MutationScalar is the mutation scalar m. See QTSim's options file format for the meaning of each field.\n\nCrossoverModel is the one field with no QTSim counterpart. QTSim draws Poisson(RecombPoissonMean) crossover points per gamete but only the lowest one switches strands (its point index never advances), so a gamete has at most one crossover. :qtsim (default) reproduces that, as the published simulations did; :all applies every drawn point (the evidently intended Poisson-crossover model). With NoQTL == 1 the two are identical.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.World","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.World","category":"type","text":"World\n\nMutable population state carried through the operators. Holds the current parents' genomes/environments, per-individual scratch (genotype/phenotype/ fitness), the survivor index set, the current selection optimum θ and width ω, and a double buffer (sgenome/senv) for the offspring generation.\n\nGenomes are stored as (NoQTL, Ploidy, PopSize); the genotype of individual i is the sum over all its loci and chromosomes (QTSim CaclGenotype).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.simulate","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.simulate","category":"function","text":"simulate(p::Params; seed=p.Seed)\n\nRun one simulation from Params, returning the recorded statistics (see run!).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.run!","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.run!","category":"function","text":"run!(w, rng; record=true)\n\nIterate the generation operator for NoGenerations, replicating QTSim's RunSim order: develop → select → reproduce → (record) → fitness-width change → mutate → swap. Returns a NamedTuple of per-sampled-generation statistics (gen, pheno_mean, pheno_var, geno_var, nsurv, theta, omega) plus extinct/extinct_gen.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.generation!","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.generation!","category":"function","text":"generation!(w, rng)\n\nOne composed generation mutate ∘ reproduce ∘ select ∘ develop, advancing the offspring into the current buffer. Provided as the standalone categorical composite (used in tests); run! inlines the same sequence plus recording and the fitness-width schedule. No-op past extinction.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.develop!","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.develop!","category":"function","text":"develop!(w)\n\nGenotype = Σ allele effects; phenotype = genotype + environment (QTSim CaclPopPhenotype). Deterministic given the environments drawn at birth.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.select!","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.select!","category":"function","text":"select!(w, rng)\n\nMove the optimum, assign each individual a stabilizing-selection fitness, and draw the survivor set (survive iff rand() < fitness). Mirrors QTSim Fitness\n\nSetSurviving. Sets w.extinct if too few survive to reproduce.\n\nFitness is the normalized Gaussian pdf(Normal(0,ω), (z−θ)/sd) / (0.4/ω), replicating QTSim exactly (the 0.4 ≈ 1/√(2π) constant included); the idealized form exp(−(z−θ)²/2ω²) is what Lean StabilizingSelection.lean proves about.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.moveoptimum!","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.moveoptimum!","category":"function","text":"Move the optimum θ if the schedule says so (QTSim SetFitnessMean).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.reproduce!","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.reproduce!","category":"function","text":"reproduce!(w, rng)\n\nFill the offspring buffer sgenome/senv by fitness-independent random mating among survivors (QTSim NewIn). Haploid: clone a random survivor. Diploid: two distinct survivors each contribute a (recombined) gamete. Offspring get a fresh environment.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.mutate!","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.mutate!","category":"function","text":"mutate!(w, rng)\n\nAdd Gaussian mutations to the offspring buffer (QTSim MutateIndividualChrNo). Each chromosome receives Poisson(L·rate) mutations, each of variance m/(ploidy·L·rate)·V_E; in expectation this injects m·V_E of variance per individual per generation (Lean mutationalVarianceInput_eq).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.gamete!","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.gamete!","category":"function","text":"gamete!(dest, chrA, chrB, rng, recombMean, L, allpoints=false)\n\nBuild one gamete from a parent's two chromosomes (QTSim CreateCombMap + RecombinationPoissonChr). Draw Poisson(recombMean) distinct crossover points in 0:L-2 (none if the count is 0 or ≥ L), start on a random parental chromosome, and switch strands after crossover points. QTSim never advances its point index, so only the LOWEST point switches: a gamete has at most one crossover. That is the default; allpoints=true switches at every point (Params CrossoverModel = :all). With L == 1 both reduce to picking one parental chromosome at random (Mendelian segregation).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.swapbuffers!","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.swapbuffers!","category":"function","text":"Swap the parent and offspring buffers (QTSim double-buffer swap).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.geneticVar","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.geneticVar","category":"function","text":"Additive genetic variance target VG = h²·VP (QTSim OptVarTarget).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.envVar","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.envVar","category":"function","text":"Environmental variance VE = VP − V_G (QTSim EnvVar).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.analyticalFitSD","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.analyticalFitSD","category":"function","text":"analyticalFitSD(p)\n\nAnalytical initial fitness-function width ω = VG / √(m·VE) (QTSim GetAnalyticalFitSD; see Lean analyticalFitSD_eq).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.initFitSD","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.initFitSD","category":"function","text":"Initial fitness width: user InitFitnessSD if ≥0, else the analytical value.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.muteVar","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.muteVar","category":"function","text":"muteVar(p)\n\nExpected per-generation mutational variance input, m·VE (QTSim MuteVar). The Poisson mutation operator is calibrated to this value; see Lean `mutationalVarianceInputeq`.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.lifecycle","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.lifecycle","category":"function","text":"lifecycle()\n\nThe life cycle as syntax: a named tuple of the Catlab presentation (presentation) of the free category whose objects are the stages of a generation (Zygotes, Adults, Survivors, Offspring), its generating steps (develop, select, reproduce, record, rescale, mutate, swap) and QTSim's generation (generation), their composite develop ⋅ select ⋅ reproduce ⋅ record ⋅ rescale ⋅ mutate ⋅ swap, a morphism Zygotes → Zygotes. Composites type-check: a step can follow another only where the stages match. lower gives a life cycle its meaning in a backend. Requires Catlab.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.lower","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.lower","category":"function","text":"lower(f, target; images = Dict(), record = nothing)\n\nThe image of the life-cycle morphism f under the lowering functor to target: :kernel gives an operator on a World, and :eidos a fragment of Eidos code for SLiM. A functor out of a free category is fixed by the images of the generators, so the image of a composite is the composite of the images. record steps in the kernel append the parent generation's statistics to record, a named tuple of vectors like the one run! returns, or discard them when it is nothing. Steps that are not in lifecycle, made with Catlab's Hom(name, dom, codom), need an image in images, keyed by name: an operator or a function (state, rng) -> state for :kernel, a string of Eidos for :eidos. Requires Catlab.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.slim_script","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.slim_script","category":"function","text":"slim_script(f, p::Params; images = Dict())\n\nA complete SLiM model, as Eidos source, that iterates the life cycle f, a morphism Zygotes → Zygotes: the image of f under lower to Eidos runs once per tick in a reproduction() callback. The parameters of p are the defaults, which slim -d \"NAME=value\" overrides (N, VP, h2, m, rate, NGEN, sampleEvery, moveGen, moveSD, moveType, changeGen, changeSD, initOmega, drift, standardise). The Eidos images of the steps cover one locus in diploids (NoQTL = 1, Ploidy = 2). Requires Catlab.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.run_composed","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.run_composed","category":"function","text":"run_composed(w::World, rng; record = true, cycle = lifecycle().generation, images = Dict())\n\nRun a simulation by applying the kernel image of the life cycle cycle, a morphism Zygotes → Zygotes, once per generation (lower; images gives the kernel images of steps that are not in lifecycle). Returns the statistics its record steps collect, as run! does; with the default life cycle, for the same seed, it is the same run as run!, bit for bit. Requires Catlab.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.operators","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.operators","category":"function","text":"operators()\n\nThe kernel images of the steps of the life cycle (lifecycle), as the named tuple (; develop, select, reproduce, record, rescale, mutate, swap, generation). An operator is called as op(world, rng), composes left to right with Catlab's ⋅ ((f ⋅ g)(w, rng) applies f then g), and acts deme by deme on a metapopulation built with ⊗. generation is the image of QTSim's generation, develop ⋅ select ⋅ reproduce ⋅ record ⋅ rescale ⋅ mutate ⋅ swap, in the order of its generation loop; here record discards what it observes (see run_composed). Requires Catlab.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.operator","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.operator","category":"function","text":"operator(name::Symbol, act; scope = :deme)\n\nA new operator that applies act(state, rng) and returns the state. With scope = :deme it acts on one population (a World) and is applied to each deme of a metapopulation; with scope = :meta it acts on the whole metapopulation, as migration does. Requires Catlab.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.demes","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.demes","category":"function","text":"demes(k)\n\nStratification into k demes: p ⊗ demes(k) with p::Params gives k independent populations, and w ⊗ demes(k) with w::World gives k copies of w. Requires Catlab.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.migrate!","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.migrate!","category":"function","text":"migrate!(s, rng; rate = 0.01)\n\nMove individuals between the demes of a metapopulation s: each individual swaps places with a random individual of a random other deme with probability rate, so the deme sizes are unchanged. Returns (; count), the number of exchanges. Requires Catlab.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.migration","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.migration","category":"function","text":"migration(rate)\n\nmigrate! as an operator on the whole metapopulation, for composing into a generation: generation ⋅ migration(0.01). Requires Catlab.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.totalpop","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.totalpop","category":"function","text":"totalpop(s)\n\nThe total number of individuals across the demes of a metapopulation. Requires Catlab.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.ndemes","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.ndemes","category":"function","text":"ndemes(s)\n\nThe number of demes of a metapopulation. Requires Catlab.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.population_schema","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.population_schema","category":"function","text":"population_schema()\n\nThe schema of the population ACSet: individuals and alleles, a map owner from each allele to the individual carrying it, allele attribute effect, and individual attributes env, genotype, phenotype and fitness. Requires Catlab.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.lift","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.lift","category":"function","text":"lift(w::World)\n\nThe parents of w as an ACSet on population_schema. Run develop! first for the genotype and phenotype attributes to be current. Requires Catlab.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.lower!","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.lower!","category":"function","text":"lower!(pop, w::World)\n\nWrite the genomes and environmental deviations of the ACSet pop into w, inverting lift. Returns w. Requires Catlab.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.genotypes","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.genotypes","category":"function","text":"genotypes(pop)\n\nEach individual's genotype, computed on the ACSet by summing the effects of the alleles in the preimage of owner (QTSim CaclGenotype). Requires Catlab.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.phenotypes","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.phenotypes","category":"function","text":"phenotypes(pop)\n\nEach individual's phenotype on the ACSet, genotype plus environmental deviation. Requires Catlab.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSim.genome_from_acset","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSim.genome_from_acset","category":"function","text":"genome_from_acset(pop, L, P)\n\nThe (L, P, N) genome array of a population ACSet, inverting lift. Requires Catlab.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSimCatlabExt","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSimCatlabExt","category":"module","text":"QTLSimCatlabExt\n\nThe categorical layer of QTLSim, loaded with Catlab. The life cycle is a morphism of a free category whose objects are the stages of a generation (lifecycle.jl), lowered by functors to the simulation kernel, where each step is an Operator composed with Catlab's ⋅ and stratified into demes with its monoidal product ⊗ (compose.jl), and to a SLiM model in Eidos (eidos.jl). The population is an attributed C-set (ACSet) on the schema of schema.jl. The facts that justify the decomposition, that composition is associative, that a run is the iterate of one composed generation and that a lowering is fixed by its action on the steps, are proved in lean/CategoricalQuantGen/Composition.lean and Lowering.lean.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSimCatlabExt.Stage","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSimCatlabExt.Stage","category":"type","text":"A stage of the life cycle as the image of an object under the lowering to backend B (:kernel or :eidos). The kernel carries every stage in one World; in Eidos, a stage is the set of variables a step can rely on (EIDOS_SCOPE).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSimCatlabExt.EidosCode","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSimCatlabExt.EidosCode","category":"type","text":"EidosCode(name, code)\n\nA fragment of Eidos, the image of a life-cycle morphism under the lowering to SLiM. The image of a composite is the concatenation of the images of its steps.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSimCatlabExt.Operator","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSimCatlabExt.Operator","category":"type","text":"Operator(name, act, scope)\n\nAn evolutionary operator: op(state, rng) applies act(state, rng). scope is :deme for an operator on one population, applied to each deme of a metapopulation, or :meta for one on the whole metapopulation (migration).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSimCatlabExt.Demes","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSimCatlabExt.Demes","category":"type","text":"Stratification into k demes, used as p ⊗ demes(k).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSimCatlabExt.Stratified","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSimCatlabExt.Stratified","category":"type","text":"A metapopulation: demes (Worlds) that operators act on one at a time.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"QTLSimCatlabExt.Population","page":"API reference","location":"api/#QTLSimCatlabExt.Population","category":"type","text":"The population ACSet, with Float64 allele effects and traits.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"Recomposing the model","page":"Recomposing the model","location":"tutorials/05_recomposing_the_model/#Recomposing-the-model","category":"section","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nSetup\nReplacing the selection operator\nDropping or repeating mutation\nInserting a new operator: a fluctuating optimum\nStratifying with ⊗ and coupling with migration"},{"title":"Overview","page":"Recomposing the model","location":"tutorials/05_recomposing_the_model/#Overview","category":"section","text":"A generation is the composite develop ⋅ select ⋅ reproduce ⋅ record ⋅ rescale ⋅ mutate ⋅ swap (The categorical layer); the variants below leave out record and rescale, which would do nothing in them. Because each step is a value, the model can be changed by changing the composite: replacing a step, dropping one, repeating one, inserting a new one, or stratifying the population into demes. The validated kernel functions are reused unchanged in every variant below; only the wiring differs."},{"title":"Setup","page":"Recomposing the model","location":"tutorials/05_recomposing_the_model/#Setup","category":"section","text":"using QTLSim\nimport Catlab                       # loads QTLSim's categorical layer\nusing Catlab: ⋅, ⊗\nusing CairoMakie, Printf, Random, Statistics\nCairoMakie.activate!(type = \"png\")\n(; develop, select, reproduce, mutate, swap, generation) = operators()\nnothing\n\nrun! runs the standard generation, and run_composed runs a life cycle written as syntax (One life cycle, two backends). For operators composed directly, as here, we write a small driver that runs any composite and records the mean phenotype and genetic variance of the parents each generation. Recording calls develop!, which uses no random numbers, so it does not disturb the run.\n\nfunction run_variant(genop, p::Params; seed)\n    rng = Xoshiro(seed); w = World(p)\n    w.env .= sqrt(envVar(p)) .* randn(rng, p.PopSize)\n    gens = Int[]; zbar = Float64[]; vg = Float64[]; θ = Float64[]\n    for g in 0:p.NoGenerations-1\n        w.gen = g\n        develop!(w)\n        push!(gens, g); push!(zbar, mean(w.phenotype)); push!(vg, var(w.genotype)); push!(θ, w.θ)\n        genop(w, rng)\n        w.extinct && break\n    end\n    (gen = gens, pheno_mean = zbar, geno_var = vg, theta = θ, extinct = w.extinct)\nend\n\nbase = Params(PopSize = 1000, Ploidy = 2, NoQTL = 1, NoGenerations = 2000,\n              PhenotypeVar = 0.02, Heritability = 0.5, MutationScalar = 0.02,\n              MutationRatePerGamete = 0.5, InitFitnessSD = 0.3)\ncolours = [:navy, :teal, :crimson]\nnothing"},{"title":"Replacing the selection operator","page":"Recomposing the model","location":"tutorials/05_recomposing_the_model/#Replacing-the-selection-operator","category":"section","text":"select is one link of the chain. Replacing it changes the selection regime while reproduction and mutation stay the same. Two alternatives: neutral drift, in which everyone survives, and truncation selection, in which only the top fifth of the population by phenotype breeds.\n\ndrift = operator(:drift, function (w, rng)\n    fill!(w.fitness, 1.0)\n    resize!(w.survivors, w.p.PopSize); w.survivors .= 1:w.p.PopSize\n    w.nsurv = w.p.PopSize\n    w\nend)\n\ntruncation(frac) = operator(:truncation, function (w, rng)\n    k = max(2, round(Int, frac * w.p.PopSize))\n    top = partialsortperm(w.phenotype, 1:k; rev = true)\n    resize!(w.survivors, k); w.survivors .= top; w.nsurv = k\n    w\nend)\n\nselection_variants = [\n    (\"stabilising\", develop ⋅ select ⋅ reproduce ⋅ mutate ⋅ swap),\n    (\"drift\", develop ⋅ drift ⋅ reproduce ⋅ mutate ⋅ swap),\n    (\"truncation (top 20%)\", develop ⋅ truncation(0.2) ⋅ reproduce ⋅ mutate ⋅ swap),\n]\nruns1 = [(name, run_variant(g, base; seed = 1)) for (name, g) in selection_variants]\nnothing\n\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 360))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"generation\", ylabel = \"mean phenotype\")\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], xlabel = \"generation\", ylabel = \"genetic variance\")\nfor ((name, r), c) in zip(runs1, colours)\n    lines!(ax1, r.gen, r.pheno_mean, color = c, label = name)\n    lines!(ax2, r.gen, r.geno_var, color = c, label = name)\nend\naxislegend(ax1, position = :lt)\nfig\n\n<div id=\"fig-selection\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 1: Replacing only the selection operator. Left: stabilising selection holds the mean at the optimum, drift lets it wander, and truncation selection drives it steadily upward. Right: genetic variance reaches a mutation–selection balance under stabilising selection, builds up under drift and is depleted by truncation.\n\n</div>"},{"title":"Dropping or repeating mutation","page":"Recomposing the model","location":"tutorials/05_recomposing_the_model/#Dropping-or-repeating-mutation","category":"section","text":"Leaving mutate out lets selection and drift use up the variance; composing it twice doubles the mutational input, and the balance rises.\n\nmutation_variants = [\n    (\"no mutation\", develop ⋅ select ⋅ reproduce ⋅ swap),\n    (\"mutation\", develop ⋅ select ⋅ reproduce ⋅ mutate ⋅ swap),\n    (\"mutation twice\", develop ⋅ select ⋅ reproduce ⋅ mutate ⋅ mutate ⋅ swap),\n]\nruns2 = [(name, run_variant(g, base; seed = 2)) for (name, g) in mutation_variants]\nfor (name, r) in runs2\n    @printf(\"%-15s mean genetic variance over the last 500 generations: %.5f\\n\",\n            name, mean(r.geno_var[end-499:end]))\nend\n\nno mutation     mean genetic variance over the last 500 generations: 0.00000\nmutation        mean genetic variance over the last 500 generations: 0.00623\nmutation twice  mean genetic variance over the last 500 generations: 0.00912\n\nfig = Figure(size = (640, 360))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"generation\", ylabel = \"genetic variance\")\nfor ((name, r), c) in zip(runs2, colours)\n    lines!(ax, r.gen, r.geno_var, color = c, label = name)\nend\naxislegend(ax, position = :rt)\nfig\n\n<div id=\"fig-mutation\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 2: Changing the mutation input by recomposition. Without mutation the genetic variance decays; with mutation applied twice per generation the balance is higher than with the standard generation.\n\n</div>"},{"title":"Inserting a new operator: a fluctuating optimum","page":"Recomposing the model","location":"tutorials/05_recomposing_the_model/#Inserting-a-new-operator:-a-fluctuating-optimum","category":"section","text":"New biology can be added as a new operator, built with operator(name, act). In QTSim the optimum either stands still or moves at a constant rate; here an operator sets it to oscillate, theta_t = A sin(2pi t  P), and is inserted before selection. (select only moves the optimum itself when MoveFitnessSDGen is set, so it leaves this one alone.)\n\nfluctuating(A, P) = operator(:fluctuate, (w, rng) -> (w.θ = A * sin(2π * w.gen / P); w))\n\nperiods = [20, 100, 500]\nruns3 = [(P, run_variant(develop ⋅ fluctuating(0.1, P) ⋅ select ⋅ reproduce ⋅ mutate ⋅ swap,\n                         base; seed = 3)) for P in periods]\nfor (P, r) in runs3\n    late = length(r.gen)-999:length(r.gen)\n    @printf(\"period %3d: the mean phenotype follows the optimum with correlation %.2f\\n\",\n            P, cor(r.pheno_mean[late], r.theta[late]))\nend\n\nperiod  20: the mean phenotype follows the optimum with correlation 0.30\nperiod 100: the mean phenotype follows the optimum with correlation 0.72\nperiod 500: the mean phenotype follows the optimum with correlation 0.97\n\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 300))\nfor (k, (P, r)) in enumerate(runs3)\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, k], title = \"period $P\", xlabel = \"generation\",\n              ylabel = k == 1 ? \"phenotype / optimum\" : \"\", limits = ((1000, 2000), (-0.15, 0.15)))\n    lines!(ax, r.gen, r.theta, color = :gray, linestyle = :dash)\n    lines!(ax, r.gen, r.pheno_mean, color = colours[k])\nend\nfig\n\n<div id=\"fig-fluctuating\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 3: A population under an optimum oscillating with amplitude 0.1 (dashed) and three periods. It tracks slow oscillations closely, lags behind intermediate ones and responds only weakly to fast ones.\n\n</div>"},{"title":"Stratifying with ⊗ and coupling with migration","page":"Recomposing the model","location":"tutorials/05_recomposing_the_model/#Stratifying-with-and-coupling-with-migration","category":"section","text":"⊗ turns one population into a metapopulation of demes, and the composed generation acts on each deme. A migration operator composed in with ⋅ couples them. Under neutral drift, isolated demes drift apart; a little migration keeps them together.\n\nfunction run_demes(genop, s, ngen; seed)\n    rng = Xoshiro(seed)\n    for d in s.demes; d.env .= sqrt(envVar(d.p)) .* randn(rng, d.p.PopSize); end\n    means = [Float64[] for _ in s.demes]\n    for g in 0:ngen-1\n        for (k, d) in enumerate(s.demes)\n            d.gen = g; develop!(d); push!(means[k], mean(d.phenotype))\n        end\n        genop(s, rng)\n    end\n    means\nend\n\nsmall = Params(PopSize = 200, Ploidy = 2, NoQTL = 1, PhenotypeVar = 0.02, Heritability = 0.5,\n               MutationScalar = 0.05, MutationRatePerGamete = 0.5)\nneutral = develop ⋅ drift ⋅ reproduce ⋅ mutate ⋅ swap\nisolated = run_demes(neutral, small ⊗ demes(4), 2500; seed = 4)\ncoupled = run_demes(neutral ⋅ migration(0.05), small ⊗ demes(4), 2500; seed = 4)\nspread(ms) = std([m[end] for m in ms])\n@printf(\"spread of the deme means after 2500 generations: isolated %.3f, with migration %.3f\\n\",\n        spread(isolated), spread(coupled))\n\nspread of the deme means after 2500 generations: isolated 0.778, with migration 0.027\n\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 360))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"generation\", ylabel = \"deme mean phenotype\", title = \"isolated\")\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], xlabel = \"generation\", title = \"with migration\")\nfor k in 1:4\n    lines!(ax1, 0:length(isolated[k])-1, isolated[k], color = (:teal, 0.8))\n    lines!(ax2, 0:length(coupled[k])-1, coupled[k], color = (:crimson, 0.8))\nend\nlinkyaxes!(ax1, ax2)\nfig\n\n<div id=\"fig-demes\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 4: Four neutral demes. Left: isolated, their means drift apart. Right: with 5% migration per generation composed into the generation, they stay together.\n\n</div>\n\nEvery variant here is a line of composition rather than a change to the simulator. That rearranging the operators is well defined, that composition is associative and a run is the iterate of one composed generation, is what lean/CategoricalQuantGen/Composition.lean proves."},{"title":"One life cycle, two backends","page":"One life cycle, two backends","location":"tutorials/06_one_life_cycle_two_backends/#One-life-cycle,-two-backends","category":"section","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nSetup\nThe life cycle as a morphism\nTwo functors\nA SLiM model from the life cycle\nThe generated model is the hand-written port\nThe generated model agrees with QTSim\nA new step, lowered twice\nSummary"},{"title":"Overview","page":"One life cycle, two backends","location":"tutorials/06_one_life_cycle_two_backends/#Overview","category":"section","text":"The previous vignettes build a generation by composing the kernel’s operators directly. Here the life cycle is written once, as syntax: a morphism of a free category whose objects are the stages of a generation and whose generating morphisms are the steps between them. A functor out of a free category is fixed by where it sends the generators, so giving each step a meaning in a backend gives every life cycle built from those steps a meaning there. Two such functors lower the life cycle: one to the simulation kernel and one to Eidos, the scripting language of the population-genetics simulator SLiM (Haller and Messer 2023).\n\nThe SLiM model lowered from QTSim’s life cycle is, run for run, the SLiM port of QTSim that was written by hand for this project, and it agrees with QTSim in distribution. A new step, given a meaning in both backends, yields two simulators of a model QTSim does not have, which can be checked against each other.\n\nThe vignette needs SLiM (version 4 or later), the hand-written port and the QTSim binary built from the paper’s code. It looks for them in slim/build/slim, slim_port/qtsim.slim and Repository/Code/QTSim/src/QTSim in the folder that holds the package’s folder; the SLIM environment variable, or a slim on the path, can stand in for the first."},{"title":"Setup","page":"One life cycle, two backends","location":"tutorials/06_one_life_cycle_two_backends/#Setup","category":"section","text":"using QTLSim\nimport Catlab                       # loads QTLSim's categorical layer\nusing Catlab: ⋅, dom, codom, Hom\nusing CairoMakie, HypothesisTests, Printf, Random, Statistics\nCairoMakie.activate!(type = \"png\")\n\nconst ROOT = normpath(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"..\"))\nconst SLIM = let built = joinpath(ROOT, \"slim\", \"build\", \"slim\")\n    haskey(ENV, \"SLIM\") ? ENV[\"SLIM\"] : isfile(built) ? built : something(Sys.which(\"slim\"), built)\nend\nconst PORT = joinpath(ROOT, \"slim_port\", \"qtsim.slim\")\nconst QTSIM = joinpath(ROOT, \"Repository\", \"Code\", \"QTSim\", \"src\", \"QTSim\")\nall(isfile, (SLIM, PORT, QTSIM)) || error(\"this vignette needs SLiM, the SLiM port and QTSim\")\nThreads.nthreads()\n\n6"},{"title":"The life cycle as a morphism","page":"One life cycle, two backends","location":"tutorials/06_one_life_cycle_two_backends/#The-life-cycle-as-a-morphism","category":"section","text":"lifecycle() returns the syntax: the Catlab presentation of a free category with four objects, the stages of a generation, and seven generating morphisms, the steps of QTSim’s generation loop.\n\nL = lifecycle()\nconst STEP_NAMES = (:develop, :select, :reproduce, :record, :rescale, :mutate, :swap)\nprintln(\"| step | from | to |\\n|:--|:--|:--|\")\nfor s in STEP_NAMES\n    f = getfield(L, s)\n    println(\"| `\", s, \"` | \", dom(f), \" | \", codom(f), \" |\")\nend\n\nstep from to\ndevelop Zygotes Adults\nselect Adults Survivors\nreproduce Survivors Offspring\nrecord Offspring Offspring\nrescale Offspring Offspring\nmutate Offspring Offspring\nswap Offspring Zygotes\n\nSteps compose with Catlab’s ⋅ when the stage one step ends in is the stage the next begins with. QTSim’s generation is the composite of all seven, in the order of its loop, and is a morphism from zygotes to zygotes:\n\n(; develop, select, reproduce, record, rescale, mutate, swap) = L\nqtsim = develop ⋅ select ⋅ reproduce ⋅ record ⋅ rescale ⋅ mutate ⋅ swap\n\ndevelop⋅select⋅reproduce⋅record⋅rescale⋅mutate⋅swap: Zygotes → Zygotes\n\nSteps whose stages do not match do not compose. Selecting before developing is not a life cycle, and Catlab says so:\n\ntry\n    select ⋅ develop\ncatch e\n    println(first(sprint(showerror, e), 120), \"…\")\nend\n\nDomain error in term constructor compose(select,develop)…"},{"title":"Two functors","page":"One life cycle, two backends","location":"tutorials/06_one_life_cycle_two_backends/#Two-functors","category":"section","text":"lower(f, :kernel) sends each step to the kernel function it wraps (develop!, select!, reproduce!, recording, the scheduled change of the fitness width, mutate!, and the swap of the parent and offspring buffers), and composition to ⋅. The image of the generation is an operator on a World:\n\nlower(qtsim, :kernel)\n\nOperator(:develop⋅select⋅reproduce⋅record⋅rescale⋅mutate⋅swap)\n\nRun once per generation by run_composed, it is run!, bit for bit, here for one locus, with the optimum moving from generation 5000 and the fitness function narrowing:\n\np = Params(PopSize = 1000, NoQTL = 1, Ploidy = 2, NoGenerations = 6500, PhenotypeVar = 0.02,\n           Heritability = 0.5, MutationScalar = 0.001, MutationRatePerGamete = 0.0002,\n           MoveFitnessSDGen = 5000, MoveFitnessSD = 1.0, MoveType = :phenotype,\n           ChangeFitnessGen = 5000, ChangeFitnessSD = 0.3)\na = run!(World(p), Xoshiro(1))\nb = run_composed(World(p), Xoshiro(1); cycle = qtsim)\nall(getfield(a, k) == getfield(b, k) for k in keys(a))\n\ntrue\n\nlower(f, :eidos) sends each step to a fragment of Eidos. Each individual carries its two allele values, as in QTSim’s continuum-of-alleles model, and SLiM supplies only the life cycle of a non-Wright–Fisher model. Selection, for example:\n\nlower(select, :eidos)\n\n    // select: move the optimum, Gaussian fitness with QTSim's 0.4, viability   [Adults → Survivors]\n    if (moveGen >= 0 & gen >= moveGen) {\n        if (moveType == \"phenotype\") defineGlobal(\"THETA\", mean(pheno) + sqrt(VP) * moveSD);\n        else if (moveType == \"fitness\") defineGlobal(\"THETA\", mean(pheno) + OMEGA * moveSD);\n        else defineGlobal(\"THETA\", THETA + moveSD);\n    }\n    x = standardise ? (pheno - THETA) / sd(pheno) else pheno - THETA;\n    W = drift ? rep(1.0, size(adults)) else dnorm(x, 0.0, OMEGA) / (0.4 / OMEGA);\n    survived = runif(size(adults)) < W;\n    sidx = which(survived);\n    ns = size(sidx);\n    if (ns < 2) { catn(\"Pop all died.\"); sim.simulationFinished(); self.active = 0; return; }\n\nComposition goes to concatenation, so the image of the generation is the seven fragments in order:\n\nlower(qtsim, :eidos).code == join(lower(getfield(L, s), :eidos).code for s in STEP_NAMES)\n\ntrue"},{"title":"A SLiM model from the life cycle","page":"One life cycle, two backends","location":"tutorials/06_one_life_cycle_two_backends/#A-SLiM-model-from-the-life-cycle","category":"section","text":"slim_script puts the image of a life cycle inside a complete SLiM model, in a reproduction() callback that runs once per tick, with the parameters of a Params as defaults that slim -d can override:\n\nscript = slim_script(qtsim, p)\nprint(script)\n\n// QTSim as a nonWF SLiM model, generated by QTLSim.jl from the life cycle\n//\n//     develop⋅select⋅reproduce⋅record⋅rescale⋅mutate⋅swap: Zygotes → Zygotes\n//\n// by the lowering to Eidos: each step of the life cycle is a fragment of the\n// reproduction() callback below, in the order of the composite. Regenerate it with\n// QTLSim's `slim_script` rather than editing it.\n//\n// Parameters can be overridden with  slim -d \"NAME=value\"  and the seed set with -s.\n\ninitialize() {\n    initializeSLiMModelType(\"nonWF\");\n\n    if (!exists(\"N\"))           defineConstant(\"N\", 1000);\n    if (!exists(\"VP\"))          defineConstant(\"VP\", 0.02);\n    if (!exists(\"h2\"))          defineConstant(\"h2\", 0.5);\n    if (!exists(\"m\"))           defineConstant(\"m\", 0.001);\n    if (!exists(\"rate\"))        defineConstant(\"rate\", 0.0002);\n    if (!exists(\"NGEN\"))        defineConstant(\"NGEN\", 6500);\n    if (!exists(\"sampleEvery\")) defineConstant(\"sampleEvery\", 1);\n    if (!exists(\"moveGen\"))     defineConstant(\"moveGen\", 5000);\n    if (!exists(\"moveSD\"))      defineConstant(\"moveSD\", 1.0);\n    if (!exists(\"moveType\"))    defineConstant(\"moveType\", \"phenotype\");\n    if (!exists(\"changeGen\"))   defineConstant(\"changeGen\", 5000);\n    if (!exists(\"changeSD\"))    defineConstant(\"changeSD\", 0.3);\n    if (!exists(\"initOmega\"))   defineConstant(\"initOmega\", -1.0);\n    if (!exists(\"drift\"))       defineConstant(\"drift\", F);\n    if (!exists(\"standardise\")) defineConstant(\"standardise\", F);\n\n    defineConstant(\"VG\", h2 * VP);\n    defineConstant(\"VE\", VP - VG);\n    defineConstant(\"ESD\", sqrt(VE));\n    defineConstant(\"MUTVAR\", (m / (2 * rate)) * VE);           // m/(Ploidy·NoQTL·rate)·V_E\n    defineConstant(\"OMEGA0\", initOmega < 0 ? VG / sqrt(m * VE) else initOmega);\n\n    // vestigial genetics: SLiM supplies only the life cycle\n    initializeMutationRate(0.0);\n    initializeMutationType(\"m1\", 0.5, \"f\", 0.0);\n    initializeGenomicElementType(\"g1\", m1, 1.0);\n    initializeGenomicElement(g1, 0, 0);\n    initializeRecombinationRate(0.0);\n}\n\n1 first() {\n    sim.addSubpop(\"p1\", N);\n    p1.individuals.setValue(\"a0\", 0.0);      // founders: allele values 0\n    p1.individuals.setValue(\"a1\", 0.0);\n    defineGlobal(\"THETA\", 0.0);\n    defineGlobal(\"OMEGA\", OMEGA0);\n}\n\n// one generation per tick, done in the first call of the callback\nreproduction() {\n    gen = sim.cycle - 1;                       // QTSim's generation index\n    // develop: genotype a0 + a1, phenotype genotype + e with e ~ N(0, V_E)   [Zygotes → Adults]\n    adults = p1.individuals;\n    a0 = adults.getValue(\"a0\");\n    a1 = adults.getValue(\"a1\");\n    genotype = a0 + a1;\n    env = rnorm(size(adults), 0.0, ESD);\n    pheno = genotype + env;\n    // select: move the optimum, Gaussian fitness with QTSim's 0.4, viability   [Adults → Survivors]\n    if (moveGen >= 0 & gen >= moveGen) {\n        if (moveType == \"phenotype\") defineGlobal(\"THETA\", mean(pheno) + sqrt(VP) * moveSD);\n        else if (moveType == \"fitness\") defineGlobal(\"THETA\", mean(pheno) + OMEGA * moveSD);\n        else defineGlobal(\"THETA\", THETA + moveSD);\n    }\n    x = standardise ? (pheno - THETA) / sd(pheno) else pheno - THETA;\n    W = drift ? rep(1.0, size(adults)) else dnorm(x, 0.0, OMEGA) / (0.4 / OMEGA);\n    survived = runif(size(adults)) < W;\n    sidx = which(survived);\n    ns = size(sidx);\n    if (ns < 2) { catn(\"Pop all died.\"); sim.simulationFinished(); self.active = 0; return; }\n    // reproduce: N offspring of two distinct survivors, a random allele from each   [Survivors → Offspring]\n    sa0 = a0[sidx]; sa1 = a1[sidx];\n    i1 = rdunif(N, 0, ns - 1);\n    i2 = rdunif(N, 0, ns - 1);\n    dup = which(i1 == i2);\n    while (size(dup) > 0) { i2[dup] = rdunif(size(dup), 0, ns - 1); dup = which(i1 == i2); }\n    c0 = ifelse(runif(N) < 0.5, sa1[i1], sa0[i1]);\n    c1 = ifelse(runif(N) < 0.5, sa1[i2], sa0[i2]);\n    // record: the parent generation's statistics, every `sampleEvery` generations   [Offspring → Offspring]\n    if (gen == 0) catn(\"gen\\tphenoMean\\tgenoVar\\tnSurv\\ttheta\\tomega\");\n    if (gen % sampleEvery == 0)\n        catn(gen + \"\\t\" + mean(pheno) + \"\\t\" + var(genotype) + \"\\t\" + ns + \"\\t\" + THETA + \"\\t\" + OMEGA);\n    // rescale: the scheduled change of the fitness width, from the next generation   [Offspring → Offspring]\n    if (gen == changeGen) defineGlobal(\"OMEGA\", changeSD);\n    // mutate: Poisson(rate) increments of N(0, MUTVAR) per offspring chromosome   [Offspring → Offspring]\n    k0 = rpois(N, rate);\n    k1 = rpois(N, rate);\n    c0 = c0 + rnorm(N, 0.0, sqrt(k0 * MUTVAR));\n    c1 = c1 + rnorm(N, 0.0, sqrt(k1 * MUTVAR));\n    // swap: the offspring replace their parents   [Offspring → Zygotes]\n    for (k in seqLen(N)) {\n        child = p1.addEmpty();\n        child.setValue(\"a0\", c0[k]);\n        child.setValue(\"a1\", c1[k]);\n    }\n    adults.fitnessScaling = 0.0;\n    if (gen >= NGEN - 1) sim.simulationFinished();\n    self.active = 0;\n}\n\n1000000 late() { sim.simulationFinished(); }   // backstop\n\nrun_slim runs a SLiM model and reads the table it prints, which has the columns QTSim’s output has.\n\nfunction run_slim(model, seed; defines = Dict{String,Any}())\n    args = [\"-s\", string(seed)]\n    for (k, v) in defines\n        push!(args, \"-d\", v isa AbstractString ? \"$k=\\\"$v\\\"\" : \"$k=$v\")\n    end\n    path = tempname() * \".slim\"; write(path, model)\n    text = try read(`$SLIM $args $path`, String) finally rm(path; force = true) end\n    rows = [split(l, '\\t') for l in split(text, '\\n') if occursin(r\"^\\d+\\t\", l)]\n    (; gen = [parse(Int, r[1]) for r in rows], pheno_mean = [parse(Float64, r[2]) for r in rows],\n       geno_var = [parse(Float64, r[3]) for r in rows], nsurv = [parse(Int, r[4]) for r in rows],\n       theta = [parse(Float64, r[5]) for r in rows], extinct = occursin(\"Pop all died.\", text),\n       lines = [join(r, '\\t') for r in rows])\nend\nnothing"},{"title":"The generated model is the hand-written port","page":"One life cycle, two backends","location":"tutorials/06_one_life_cycle_two_backends/#The-generated-model-is-the-hand-written-port","category":"section","text":"The project also has a SLiM port of QTSim written by hand, slim_port/qtsim.slim, validated against QTSim before the life cycle existed. Run with the same seed and parameters, the generated model and the port print the same table, number for number, because their steps are the same Eidos statements in the same order and so draw the same random numbers. The one difference is intended: when a population dies, the port also prints the generation in which it died, and QTSim does not.\n\nport = read(PORT, String)\nchecks = (\n    (\"stabilising selection\", Dict{String,Any}(\"N\" => 1000, \"VP\" => 0.02, \"h2\" => 0.5, \"m\" => 0.02,\n        \"rate\" => 0.5, \"NGEN\" => 600, \"moveGen\" => -1, \"changeGen\" => -1, \"changeSD\" => -1.0, \"initOmega\" => 0.3)),\n    (\"moving optimum\", Dict{String,Any}(\"N\" => 1000, \"VP\" => 0.02, \"h2\" => 0.5, \"m\" => 0.001,\n        \"rate\" => 0.0002, \"NGEN\" => 600, \"moveGen\" => 300, \"moveSD\" => 1.0, \"changeGen\" => 300,\n        \"changeSD\" => 0.3, \"initOmega\" => -1.0)),\n    (\"extinction\", Dict{String,Any}(\"N\" => 300, \"VP\" => 0.02, \"h2\" => 0.5, \"m\" => 0.001,\n        \"rate\" => 0.0002, \"NGEN\" => 600, \"moveGen\" => 50, \"moveSD\" => 6.0, \"changeGen\" => 50,\n        \"changeSD\" => 0.05, \"initOmega\" => -1.0)))\nprintln(\"| scenario | seeds | identical tables | extinct |\\n|:--|--:|--:|--:|\")\nfor (name, d) in checks\n    same = Threads.Atomic{Int}(0); died = Threads.Atomic{Int}(0)\n    Threads.@threads for seed in 1:10\n        a = run_slim(port, seed; defines = d); b = run_slim(script, seed; defines = d)\n        ref = a.extinct ? a.lines[1:end-1] : a.lines      # the port's extra line at extinction\n        Threads.atomic_add!(same, Int(a.extinct == b.extinct && ref == b.lines))\n        Threads.atomic_add!(died, Int(b.extinct))\n    end\n    println(\"| \", name, \" | 10 | \", same[], \" | \", died[], \" |\")\nend\n\nscenario seeds identical tables extinct\nstabilising selection 10 10 0\nmoving optimum 10 10 0\nextinction 10 10 10"},{"title":"The generated model agrees with QTSim","page":"One life cycle, two backends","location":"tutorials/06_one_life_cycle_two_backends/#The-generated-model-agrees-with-QTSim","category":"section","text":"SLiM and QTSim draw different random numbers, so they can agree only in distribution. Each of three scenarios is run 50 times by QTSim, by the SLiM model generated from the life cycle, and by the kernel’s image of the same life cycle. Scenarios A and B are those of Validation against QTSim: a sharp mutation–selection balance, and an optimum that keeps a fixed lead over the mean phenotype from generation 5000 while the fitness function narrows. In scenario C the optimum moves by a fixed amount each generation from generation 5000, as in the paper’s simulations. The hand-written port has no continuous move; the generated model has it because the selection step does.\n\nscen = (\n    A = Params(PopSize = 1000, NoQTL = 1, Ploidy = 2, NoGenerations = 4000, PhenotypeVar = 0.02,\n               Heritability = 0.5, MutationScalar = 0.02, MutationRatePerGamete = 0.5, InitFitnessSD = 0.3),\n    B = p,\n    C = Params(p; MoveType = :continuous, MoveFitnessSD = 0.002, ChangeFitnessGen = -1))\n\nfunction write_qtsim_options(path, q::Params, seed)\n    open(path, \"w\") do io\n        println(io, \"Sample $(q.Sample)\\nPopSize $(q.PopSize)\\nNoQTL $(q.NoQTL)\")\n        println(io, \"Generations $(q.NoGenerations)\\nPloidy $(q.Ploidy)\")\n        q.MoveFitnessSDGen != -1 && println(io, \"MoveFitnessSD $(q.MoveFitnessSDGen) $(q.MoveFitnessSD)\")\n        q.ChangeFitnessGen != -1 && println(io, \"ChangeFitnessSD $(q.ChangeFitnessGen) $(q.ChangeFitnessSD)\")\n        println(io, \"OptimumMoveType $(q.MoveType)\\nPhenotypeVar $(q.PhenotypeVar)\")\n        println(io, \"MutationScalar $(q.MutationScalar)\\nHeritability $(q.Heritability)\")\n        println(io, \"MutationRatePerGamete $(q.MutationRatePerGamete)\")\n        q.InitFitnessSD >= 0 && println(io, \"InitFitnessSD $(q.InitFitnessSD)\")\n        println(io, \"Seed $seed\")\n    end\nend\n\nfunction run_qtsim(q::Params, seed)\n    opts = tempname() * \".txt\"; write_qtsim_options(opts, q, seed)\n    text = try read(`$QTSIM $opts`, String) finally rm(opts; force = true) end\n    gen = Int[]; zbar = Float64[]; vg = Float64[]; ns = Int[]; θ = Float64[]; inbody = false\n    for line in split(text, '\\n')\n        startswith(line, \"Pop all died\") &&\n            return (; gen, pheno_mean = zbar, geno_var = vg, nsurv = ns, theta = θ, extinct = true)\n        startswith(line, \"Generation\\t\") && (inbody = true; continue)\n        (inbody && !isempty(strip(line))) || continue\n        f = split(line, '\\t')\n        push!(gen, parse(Int, f[1])); push!(zbar, parse(Float64, f[2])); push!(vg, parse(Float64, f[4]))\n        push!(ns, parse(Int, f[5])); push!(θ, parse(Float64, f[6]))\n    end\n    (; gen, pheno_mean = zbar, geno_var = vg, nsurv = ns, theta = θ, extinct = false)\nend\n\nconst R = 50\nprograms = (:QTSim, :SLiM, :kernel)\nmodels = map(q -> slim_script(qtsim, q), scen)\njobs = [(prog, s, k) for prog in programs for s in keys(scen) for k in 1:R]\nruns = Vector{Any}(undef, length(jobs))\nThreads.@threads :greedy for j in eachindex(jobs)\n    prog, s, k = jobs[j]\n    q = scen[s]\n    runs[j] = prog === :QTSim ? run_qtsim(q, 1000k) :\n              prog === :SLiM ? run_slim(models[s], 1000k + 1) :\n              run_composed(World(q), Xoshiro(1000k + 2); cycle = qtsim)\nend\nresult(prog, s) = [runs[j] for j in eachindex(jobs) if jobs[j][1] === prog && jobs[j][2] === s]\nnothing\n\nThe summary statistics are those of the validation vignette: the genetic variance at equilibrium or at the end, the fraction of each generation that survives selection after the move, and how far the mean phenotype advances. The table gives their medians over the runs, and the p-values of two-sample Kolmogorov–Smirnov tests against QTSim.\n\nwindow(r, lo, hi) = findall(g -> lo <= g <= hi, r.gen)\nstatistics = (\n    (:A, \"equilibrium genetic variance\", r -> mean(r.geno_var[window(r, 3000, 3999)])),\n    (:B, \"genetic variance at the end\", r -> mean(r.geno_var[window(r, 6400, 6499)])),\n    (:B, \"survival after the move\", r -> mean(r.nsurv[window(r, 5000, 6499)]) / 1000),\n    (:B, \"advance of the mean phenotype\", r -> mean(r.pheno_mean[window(r, 6400, 6499)]) - mean(r.pheno_mean[window(r, 5000, 5010)])),\n    (:C, \"genetic variance at the end\", r -> mean(r.geno_var[window(r, 6400, 6499)])),\n    (:C, \"survival after the move\", r -> mean(r.nsurv[window(r, 5000, 6499)]) / 1000),\n    (:C, \"advance of the mean phenotype\", r -> mean(r.pheno_mean[window(r, 6400, 6499)]) - mean(r.pheno_mean[window(r, 5000, 5010)])))\nksp(a, b) = pvalue(ApproximateTwoSampleKSTest(a, b))\nprintln(\"| scenario | statistic | QTSim | SLiM (generated) | kernel | KS p, SLiM | KS p, kernel |\")\nprintln(\"|:--|:--|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|\")\nfor (s, name, f) in statistics\n    v = Dict(prog => [f(r) for r in result(prog, s) if !r.extinct] for prog in programs)\n    @printf(\"| %s | %s | %.4g | %.4g | %.4g | %.2f | %.2f |\\n\", s, name,\n            median(v[:QTSim]), median(v[:SLiM]), median(v[:kernel]), ksp(v[:SLiM], v[:QTSim]), ksp(v[:kernel], v[:QTSim]))\nend\n@printf(\"\\nExtinctions: %s.\\n\", join((\"$prog $(sum(r.extinct for s in keys(scen) for r in result(prog, s)))\" for prog in programs), \", \"))\n\nscenario statistic QTSim SLiM (generated) kernel KS p, SLiM KS p, kernel\nA equilibrium genetic variance 0.006416 0.006426 0.006412 0.86 0.96\nB genetic variance at the end 0.00301 0.002083 0.002459 0.04 0.27\nB survival after the move 0.8469 0.8475 0.8475 0.39 0.27\nB advance of the mean phenotype 4.533 4.343 4.198 0.02 0.18\nC genetic variance at the end 0.008948 0.007413 0.007797 0.39 0.27\nC survival after the move 0.9428 0.9382 0.9382 0.86 0.71\nC advance of the mean phenotype 1.347 1.315 1.425 0.96 0.71\n\nExtinctions: QTSim 0, SLiM 0, kernel 0.\n\nTwelve of the fourteen comparisons are comfortably consistent. The two below 0.05 are both for the SLiM model in scenario B: the genetic variance at the end of the run and the advance of the mean phenotype, which depends on it. With fourteen tests one or two such p-values are not unusual, and these are the noisiest statistics here: in scenario B about 0.4 new mutations enter the population each generation, each with a standard deviation of 0.16, more than the phenotypic standard deviation of 0.14, so the genetic variance late in a run rests on a handful of them. Ten times as many runs of scenario B, with new seeds, settle it:\n\nconst RB = 500\njobs_b = [(prog, k) for prog in programs for k in 1:RB]\nruns_b = Vector{Any}(undef, length(jobs_b))\nThreads.@threads :greedy for j in eachindex(jobs_b)\n    prog, k = jobs_b[j]\n    runs_b[j] = prog === :QTSim ? run_qtsim(scen.B, 170_000 + k) :\n                prog === :SLiM ? run_slim(models.B, 180_000 + k) :\n                run_composed(World(scen.B), Xoshiro(190_000 + k); cycle = qtsim)\nend\nprintln(\"| scenario B, $RB runs each | QTSim | SLiM (generated) | kernel | KS p, SLiM | KS p, kernel |\")\nprintln(\"|:--|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|\")\nfor (s, name, f) in statistics\n    s === :B || continue\n    v = Dict(prog => [f(runs_b[j]) for j in eachindex(jobs_b) if jobs_b[j][1] === prog && !runs_b[j].extinct]\n             for prog in programs)\n    @printf(\"| %s | %.4g | %.4g | %.4g | %.2f | %.2f |\\n\", name, median(v[:QTSim]), median(v[:SLiM]),\n            median(v[:kernel]), ksp(v[:SLiM], v[:QTSim]), ksp(v[:kernel], v[:QTSim]))\nend\n\nscenario B, 500 runs each QTSim SLiM (generated) kernel KS p, SLiM KS p, kernel\ngenetic variance at the end 0.002494 0.002438 0.002465 0.86 0.67\nsurvival after the move 0.8473 0.8476 0.8474 0.08 0.56\nadvance of the mean phenotype 4.345 4.242 4.339 0.11 0.99\n\nWith ten times the runs nothing separates the three programs: the two low p-values were chance.\n\nfig = Figure(size = (720, 380))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"generation\", ylabel = \"lag of the mean phenotype, θ − z̄\")\nfor (prog, c) in zip(programs, (:gray30, :crimson, :navy))\n    rs = filter(r -> !r.extinct, result(prog, :C))\n    @assert all(r -> r.gen == 0:6499, rs)                # one row per generation\n    gens = 4900:6499\n    lag = [mean(r.theta[g + 1] - r.pheno_mean[g + 1] for r in rs) for g in gens]\n    lines!(ax, gens, lag, color = c, label = string(prog))\nend\naxislegend(ax, position = :lt)\nfig\n\n<div id=\"fig-lag\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 1: Scenario C: how far the mean phenotype lags behind the optimum after the optimum starts to move at generation 5000, averaged over the 50 runs of each program.\n\n</div>\n\nIn scenario C the three programs’ lag curves lie on top of one another: the mean phenotype falls further and further behind the moving optimum, ever more slowly as selection against the lag strengthens."},{"title":"A new step, lowered twice","page":"One life cycle, two backends","location":"tutorials/06_one_life_cycle_two_backends/#A-new-step,-lowered-twice","category":"section","text":"A step that QTSim does not have, an optimum that fluctuates from generation to generation, is a new generator: Catlab’s Hom gives it a name and the stages it connects, here adults to adults, between development and selection. A life cycle that uses it type-checks like any other:\n\nAdults = codom(develop)\nfluctuate = Hom(:fluctuate, Adults, Adults)\nnoisy = develop ⋅ fluctuate ⋅ select ⋅ reproduce ⋅ record ⋅ rescale ⋅ mutate ⋅ swap\n\ndevelop⋅fluctuate⋅select⋅reproduce⋅record⋅rescale⋅mutate⋅swap: Zygotes → Zygotes\n\nThe lowering functors know nothing of fluctuate, so each needs its image: in the kernel, a function that draws a new optimum; in Eidos, the statement that does the same.\n\nσθ = 0.15\nimages = (kernel = Dict(:fluctuate => (w, rng) -> (w.θ = σθ * randn(rng); w)),\n          eidos = Dict(:fluctuate => \"\"\"\n              // fluctuate: a new optimum each generation   [Adults → Adults]\n              defineGlobal(\"THETA\", rnorm(1, 0.0, $σθ));\n          \"\"\"))\nnoisy_model = slim_script(noisy, scen.A; images = images.eidos)\nprint(noisy_model[findfirst(\"// develop\", noisy_model).start:findfirst(\"// reproduce\", noisy_model).start-1])\n\n// develop: genotype a0 + a1, phenotype genotype + e with e ~ N(0, V_E)   [Zygotes → Adults]\n    adults = p1.individuals;\n    a0 = adults.getValue(\"a0\");\n    a1 = adults.getValue(\"a1\");\n    genotype = a0 + a1;\n    env = rnorm(size(adults), 0.0, ESD);\n    pheno = genotype + env;\n    // fluctuate: a new optimum each generation   [Adults → Adults]\n    defineGlobal(\"THETA\", rnorm(1, 0.0, 0.15));\n    // select: move the optimum, Gaussian fitness with QTSim's 0.4, viability   [Adults → Survivors]\n    if (moveGen >= 0 & gen >= moveGen) {\n        if (moveType == \"phenotype\") defineGlobal(\"THETA\", mean(pheno) + sqrt(VP) * moveSD);\n        else if (moveType == \"fitness\") defineGlobal(\"THETA\", mean(pheno) + OMEGA * moveSD);\n        else defineGlobal(\"THETA\", THETA + moveSD);\n    }\n    x = standardise ? (pheno - THETA) / sd(pheno) else pheno - THETA;\n    W = drift ? rep(1.0, size(adults)) else dnorm(x, 0.0, OMEGA) / (0.4 / OMEGA);\n    survived = runif(size(adults)) < W;\n    sidx = which(survived);\n    ns = size(sidx);\n    if (ns < 2) { catn(\"Pop all died.\"); sim.simulationFinished(); self.active = 0; return; }\n\nWith the optimum’s standard deviation at 0.15, about one phenotypic standard deviation, the two backends are two independent simulators of the new model. Each is run 50 times under scenario A, with the constant optimum’s runs from above for comparison (medians, and the p-value of a Kolmogorov–Smirnov test between the backends):\n\nnoisy_runs = Vector{Any}(undef, 2R)\nThreads.@threads :greedy for j in 1:2R\n    noisy_runs[j] = j <= R ? run_slim(noisy_model, 5000 + j) :\n                    run_composed(World(scen.A), Xoshiro(5000 + j); cycle = noisy, images = images.kernel)\nend\nvg(r) = mean(r.geno_var[window(r, 3000, 3999)])\nsv(r) = mean(r.nsurv[window(r, 3000, 3999)]) / 1000\nprintln(\"| life cycle | statistic | SLiM | kernel | KS p |\\n|:--|:--|--:|--:|--:|\")\nfor (name, f) in ((\"equilibrium genetic variance\", vg), (\"survival\", sv))\n    for (cycle, sl, ke) in ((\"constant optimum\", result(:SLiM, :A), result(:kernel, :A)),\n                            (\"fluctuating optimum\", noisy_runs[1:R], noisy_runs[R+1:end]))\n        xs = f.(sl); ys = f.(ke)\n        @printf(\"| %s | %s | %.4g | %.4g | %.2f |\\n\", cycle, name, median(xs), median(ys), ksp(xs, ys))\n    end\nend\n# Gaussian selection: E[W] = c ω / √(ω² + V_P + σθ²), with QTSim's c = 1/(0.4√(2π))\npredicted(vgeq, σ2) = scen.A.InitFitnessSD / sqrt(scen.A.InitFitnessSD^2 + vgeq + envVar(scen.A) + σ2) /\n                      (0.4 * sqrt(2π))\n@printf(\"\\nGaussian selection predicts survival %.3f with the constant optimum and %.3f with the fluctuating one, at the genetic variance the kernel's runs reach.\\n\",\n        predicted(median(vg.(result(:kernel, :A))), 0.0), predicted(median(vg.(noisy_runs[R+1:end])), σθ^2))\n\nlife cycle statistic SLiM kernel KS p\nconstant optimum equilibrium genetic variance 0.006426 0.006412 0.39\nfluctuating optimum equilibrium genetic variance 0.006429 0.006386 0.27\nconstant optimum survival 0.9169 0.9169 0.86\nfluctuating optimum survival 0.8306 0.8299 0.18\n\nGaussian selection predicts survival 0.917 with the constant optimum and 0.833 with the fluctuating one, at the genetic variance the kernel’s runs reach.\n\nThe two backends agree with each other, and both match what Gaussian selection predicts: a fluctuating optimum adds its variance to the spread of phenotypes about the optimum, and so lowers survival."},{"title":"Summary","page":"One life cycle, two backends","location":"tutorials/06_one_life_cycle_two_backends/#Summary","category":"section","text":"QTSim’s life cycle is written once, as the morphism develop ⋅ select ⋅ reproduce ⋅ record ⋅ rescale ⋅ mutate ⋅ swap of a free category, and two functors lower it: to the simulation kernel, where it is run! bit for bit, and to Eidos, where it is the hand-written SLiM port run for run, in agreement with QTSim in distribution, including the continuous move of the optimum that the port never had. A new step needs one image per backend and nothing else. That a lowering is fixed by its images on the steps, and that for Markov kernels two lowerings whose steps agree in distribution give the whole generation the same distribution, is proved in Lean in lean/CategoricalQuantGen/Lowering.lean.\n\n<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-Haller2023\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nHaller, Benjamin C., and Philipp W. Messer. 2023. “SLiM 4: Multispecies Eco-Evolutionary Modeling.” The American Naturalist 201 (5): E127–39. https://doi.org/10.1086/723601.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"title":"Validation against QTSim","page":"Validation against QTSim","location":"tutorials/08_validation_against_qtsim/#Validation-against-QTSim","category":"section","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nSetup\nStatistical parity\nExact parity\nThe paper’s published results"},{"title":"Overview","page":"Validation against QTSim","location":"tutorials/08_validation_against_qtsim/#Overview","category":"section","text":"The package is meant to be the paper’s model, not an approximation of it. This vignette collects the evidence at three strengths:\n\nStatistical: the Julia model and QTSim, run many times with  their own random numbers, produce the same distributions of the  quantities the paper studies.\nExact: a replica that draws its random numbers from the same  library routines as QTSim, in the same order, reproduces QTSim’s  output generation by generation.\nPublished: on the paper’s own inputs, the Julia model reproduces  the paper’s published per-run results.\n\nThe first two need the QTSim program, and the replica needs the GSL library QTSim is built on. They look for QTSim in a Repository/ folder next to the package’s folder, which is where the paper’s code sits in the project this package comes from, and are skipped if it is not there. The third uses results stored with the package."},{"title":"Setup","page":"Validation against QTSim","location":"tutorials/08_validation_against_qtsim/#Setup","category":"section","text":"using QTLSim\nusing CairoMakie, DelimitedFiles, HypothesisTests, Printf, Random, Statistics\nCairoMakie.activate!(type = \"png\")\n\nconst PKG = normpath(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\"))\nconst QTSIM_BIN = normpath(joinpath(PKG, \"..\", \"Repository\", \"Code\", \"QTSim\", \"src\", \"QTSim\"))\nhave_qtsim = isfile(QTSIM_BIN)\n\ntrue"},{"title":"Statistical parity","page":"Validation against QTSim","location":"tutorials/08_validation_against_qtsim/#Statistical-parity","category":"section","text":"Two scenarios with one locus, the protocol of the input-generator script shipped with QTSim. In scenario A the population sits at a sharp mutation–selection balance; in scenario B its optimum starts moving at generation 5000 (MoveType = :phenotype) and the fitness function narrows. Each is run 100 times by each program, and the distributions of five summary statistics are compared with two-sample Kolmogorov–Smirnov tests.\n\nQTSim is driven through its options file, and its output is parsed into the same shape as simulate’s.\n\nfunction write_qtsim_options(path, q::Params, seed)\n    open(path, \"w\") do io\n        println(io, \"Sample $(q.Sample)\\nPopSize $(q.PopSize)\\nNoQTL $(q.NoQTL)\")\n        println(io, \"Generations $(q.NoGenerations)\\nPloidy $(q.Ploidy)\")\n        q.MoveFitnessSDGen != -1 && println(io, \"MoveFitnessSD $(q.MoveFitnessSDGen) $(q.MoveFitnessSD)\")\n        q.ChangeFitnessGen != -1 && println(io, \"ChangeFitnessSD $(q.ChangeFitnessGen) $(q.ChangeFitnessSD)\")\n        println(io, \"OptimumMoveType $(q.MoveType)\\nPhenotypeVar $(q.PhenotypeVar)\")\n        println(io, \"MutationScalar $(q.MutationScalar)\\nHeritability $(q.Heritability)\")\n        println(io, \"MutationRatePerGamete $(q.MutationRatePerGamete)\")\n        println(io, \"RecombinationPoissonMean $(q.RecombPoissonMean)\")\n        q.InitFitnessSD >= 0 && println(io, \"InitFitnessSD $(q.InitFitnessSD)\")\n        println(io, \"Seed $seed\")\n    end\nend\n\nfunction run_qtsim(q::Params, seed)\n    opts = tempname() * \".txt\"; write_qtsim_options(opts, q, seed)\n    text = try read(`$QTSIM_BIN $opts`, String) finally rm(opts; force = true) end\n    gen = Int[]; zbar = Float64[]; vg = Float64[]; ns = Int[]; inbody = false\n    for line in split(text, '\\n')\n        startswith(line, \"Pop all died\") &&\n            return (; gen, pheno_mean = zbar, geno_var = vg, nsurv = ns, extinct = true)\n        startswith(line, \"Generation\\t\") && (inbody = true; continue)   # the column header\n        (inbody && !isempty(strip(line))) || continue\n        f = split(line, '\\t')\n        push!(gen, parse(Int, f[1])); push!(zbar, parse(Float64, f[2]))\n        push!(vg, parse(Float64, f[4])); push!(ns, parse(Int, f[5]))\n    end\n    (; gen, pheno_mean = zbar, geno_var = vg, nsurv = ns, extinct = false)\nend\n\nA = Params(Sample = 10, PopSize = 1000, Ploidy = 2, NoQTL = 1, NoGenerations = 4000,\n           PhenotypeVar = 0.02, Heritability = 0.5, MutationScalar = 0.02,\n           MutationRatePerGamete = 0.5, InitFitnessSD = 0.3)\nB = Params(Sample = 1, PopSize = 1000, Ploidy = 2, NoQTL = 1, NoGenerations = 6500,\n           PhenotypeVar = 0.02, Heritability = 0.5, MutationScalar = 0.001,\n           ChangeFitnessGen = 5000, ChangeFitnessSD = 0.30,\n           MoveFitnessSDGen = 5000, MoveFitnessSD = 1.0, MoveType = :phenotype)\nR = 100\nnothing\n\nThe summary statistics are applied to both programs’ output alike. Under the phenotype move the lag theta - bar z is fixed by construction, so instead of the lag we compare how far the mean phenotype advances.\n\nwindow(r, lo, hi) = findall(g -> lo <= g <= hi, r.gen)\nmetrics_A(r) = (; equilibrium_VG = mean(r.geno_var[window(r, 3000, 3999)]))\nmetrics_B(r) = (; end_VG = mean(r.geno_var[window(r, 6400, 6499)]),\n                  mean_VG_before_move = mean(r.geno_var[window(r, 1000, 4999)]),\n                  survival = mean(r.nsurv[window(r, 5000, 6499)]) / B.PopSize,\n                  advance = mean(r.pheno_mean[window(r, 6400, 6499)]) -\n                            mean(r.pheno_mean[window(r, 5000, 5010)]))\nstats = [(:A, :equilibrium_VG), (:B, :end_VG), (:B, :mean_VG_before_move),\n         (:B, :survival), (:B, :advance)]\n\nout = Any[]\nif have_qtsim\n    jobs = [(prog, scen, s) for prog in (:julia, :qtsim) for scen in (:A, :B) for s in 1:R]\n    out = Vector{Any}(undef, length(jobs))\n    Threads.@threads for k in eachindex(jobs)\n        prog, scen, s = jobs[k]\n        q = scen === :A ? A : B\n        r = prog === :julia ? simulate(q; seed = 10_000 + s) : run_qtsim(q, 20_000 + s)\n        out[k] = (; prog, scen, extinct = r.extinct,\n                  m = r.extinct ? nothing : (scen === :A ? metrics_A(r) : metrics_B(r)))\n    end\nend\npick(prog, scen, f) = [getfield(o.m, f) for o in out\n                       if o.prog === prog && o.scen === scen && !o.extinct]\nnothing\n\nif have_qtsim\n    println(\"| scenario | statistic | Julia mean | QTSim mean | KS D | KS p |\")\n    println(\"|:--|:--|--:|--:|--:|--:|\")\n    for (scen, f) in stats\n        j, c = pick(:julia, scen, f), pick(:qtsim, scen, f)\n        t = ApproximateTwoSampleKSTest(j, c)\n        @printf(\"| %s | %s | %.4g | %.4g | %.3f | %.2f |\\n\", scen, f, mean(j), mean(c), t.δ, pvalue(t))\n    end\n    @printf(\"\\nExtinctions in scenario B: Julia %d of %d, QTSim %d of %d.\\n\",\n            count(o -> o.prog === :julia && o.scen === :B && o.extinct, out), R,\n            count(o -> o.prog === :qtsim && o.scen === :B && o.extinct, out), R)\nelse\n    println(\"QTSim was not found at `$QTSIM_BIN`, so this section was skipped.\")\nend\n\nscenario statistic Julia mean QTSim mean KS D KS p\nA equilibrium_VG 0.006411 0.006423 0.110 0.58\nB end_VG 0.002667 0.002599 0.150 0.21\nB meanVGbefore_move 0.003579 0.004113 0.140 0.28\nB survival 0.8472 0.8475 0.110 0.58\nB advance 4.365 4.251 0.110 0.58\n\nExtinctions in scenario B: Julia 0 of 100, QTSim 0 of 100.\n\nif have_qtsim\n    fig = Figure(size = (1000, 560))\n    for (k, (scen, f)) in enumerate(stats)\n        ax = Axis(fig[fldmod1(k, 3)...], xlabel = \"$f ($scen)\", ylabel = \"cumulative fraction\")\n        ecdfplot!(ax, pick(:julia, scen, f), color = :teal, label = \"Julia\")\n        ecdfplot!(ax, pick(:qtsim, scen, f), color = :crimson, label = \"QTSim\")\n        k == 1 && axislegend(ax, position = :rb)\n    end\n    fig\nend\n\n<div id=\"fig-ecdf\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 1: Empirical distribution functions of the summary statistics, Julia (teal) against QTSim (crimson), 100 runs each.\n\n</div>\n\nThe distributions coincide. The genetic-variance statistics of scenario B, a single locus under weak selection, are heavy-tailed and noisy even with 100 runs; scenario A’s equilibrium variance is the sharp test of the same quantity."},{"title":"Exact parity","page":"Validation against QTSim","location":"tutorials/08_validation_against_qtsim/#Exact-parity","category":"section","text":"Statistical agreement leaves room for small systematic differences hidden by noise. To rule them out, validate/gsl_locked.jl contains a replica of the model that draws every random number from the GNU Scientific Library routines QTSim uses, in QTSim’s order, and sums genotypes in QTSim’s order. Given QTSim’s seed it should reproduce QTSim’s printed output exactly: the mean phenotype and genetic variance to all six printed decimals and the number of survivors, every generation. compare runs both and reports any generation that differs. The checks cover the single-locus protocol and one of the paper’s own 100-locus input files, which runs until the population dies; a negative control applies every crossover point, which QTSim does not, and must diverge.\n\ninclude(joinpath(PKG, \"validate\", \"gsl_locked.jl\"))   # defines the replica; runs nothing\n\nif have_qtsim && isfile(LIBGSL)\n    compare(B, 12345, \"single-locus protocol, seed 12345\")\n    q, seed = read_qtsim_opts(joinpath(SIMDIR, \"QT0100-Possion3.0\", \"Input-00001.txt\"))\n    compare(Params(q; NoGenerations = 10_600), seed, \"QT0100-Possion3.0/Input-00001.txt\")\n    compare(Params(q; NoGenerations = 2_000), seed, \"same input, every crossover point\";\n            allpoints = true)\nelse\n    println(\"QTSim or the GSL library was not found, so this section was skipped.\")\nend\n\nsingle-locus protocol, seed 12345            gens compared=6500   mismatches=0    survived (replica extinct=false)\n  ⇒ BIT-IDENTICAL (6 dp + survivor counts, every generation)\nQT0100-Possion3.0/Input-00001.txt            gens compared=10168  mismatches=0    extinct at gen 10168 (replica 10168)\n  ⇒ BIT-IDENTICAL (6 dp + survivor counts, every generation)\nsame input, every crossover point            gens compared=2000   mismatches=1998 survived (replica extinct=false)\n  ⇒ DIVERGES at gen 2\n\nfalse\n\nThe replica is bit-identical to QTSim, and the negative control diverges at once. The replica’s random numbers come from the same routines as QTSim’s, while the package’s kernel uses Julia’s own generators, so the kernel can only be compared statistically; the replica shows that the algorithm is QTSim’s."},{"title":"The paper’s published results","page":"Validation against QTSim","location":"tutorials/08_validation_against_qtsim/#The-paper’s-published-results","category":"section","text":"The strongest check uses the paper’s own inputs. validate/published_sweep.jl drew 589 of the paper’s 119,984 input files, about 75 from each of the eight strata of locus count and recombination rate, and ran each with the Julia model under both crossover models (only QTSim’s at 5000 loci, where runs are slowest), stopping 2000 generations after the optimum starts to move. (It also confirms that QTSim reproduces the published table exactly on the inputs it reruns.) Its results are stored with the package, next to the published values for the same inputs.\n\ndata, header = readdlm(joinpath(PKG, \"validate\", \"published_sweep_results.csv\"), ',', header = true)\nnum(x) = x isa Real ? Float64(x) : something(tryparse(Float64, String(x)), NaN)   # empty cells → NaN\ncol(name) = num.(data[:, findfirst(==(name), vec(header))])\ncens(T) = min.(T, 2000.0)                               # survival after the move, capped\npub_VG, pub_T = col(\"pub_gv10000\"), cens(col(\"pub_T\"))\n\nprintln(\"| crossover model | quantity | Julia / published | 95% interval |\")\nprintln(\"|:--|:--|--:|--:|\")\nfor (model, label) in ((\"qtsim\", \"QTSim (`:qtsim`)\"), (\"all\", \"every point (`:all`)\"))\n    for (name, jl, pub) in ((\"genetic variance before the move\", col(\"jl_$(model)_gv10000\"), pub_VG),\n                            (\"generations to extinction, capped at 2000\", cens(col(\"jl_$(model)_Tcens\")), pub_T))\n        ok = isfinite.(jl) .& isfinite.(pub) .& (jl .> 0) .& (pub .> 0)\n        d = log.(jl[ok]) .- log.(pub[ok])\n        m, se = mean(d), std(d) / sqrt(length(d))\n        @printf(\"| %s | %s | %.3f | %.3f–%.3f (%d inputs) |\\n\", label, name, exp(m), exp(m - 1.96se), exp(m + 1.96se), length(d))\n    end\nend\n\ncrossover model quantity Julia / published 95% interval\nQTSim (:qtsim) genetic variance before the move 1.007 0.986–1.029 (589 inputs)\nQTSim (:qtsim) generations to extinction, capped at 2000 1.008 0.990–1.026 (589 inputs)\nevery point (:all) genetic variance before the move 1.056 1.035–1.077 (565 inputs)\nevery point (:all) generations to extinction, capped at 2000 1.123 1.094–1.152 (565 inputs)\n\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 400))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], xscale = log10, yscale = log10, xlabel = \"published\", ylabel = \"Julia\",\n           title = \"genetic variance before the move\")\njv = col(\"jl_qtsim_gv10000\"); ok = isfinite.(jv) .& isfinite.(pub_VG) .& (jv .> 0) .& (pub_VG .> 0)\nlines!(ax1, [1e-4, 1], [1e-4, 1], color = :gray, linestyle = :dash)\nscatter!(ax1, pub_VG[ok], jv[ok], color = (:teal, 0.5), markersize = 6)\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], xscale = log10, yscale = log10, xlabel = \"published\", ylabel = \"Julia\",\n           title = \"generations to extinction\")\njT = cens(col(\"jl_qtsim_Tcens\"))\nlines!(ax2, [1, 2000], [1, 2000], color = :gray, linestyle = :dash)\nscatter!(ax2, pub_T, jT, color = (:crimson, 0.5), markersize = 6)\nfig\n\n<div id=\"fig-published\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 2: The Julia model (QTSim’s crossover model) against the paper’s published results for the same 589 inputs. Left: genetic variance just before the optimum starts moving. Right: generations to extinction after the move, capped at 2000. The lines are equality.\n\n</div>\n\nIndividual runs scatter around the line, as they must: the Julia model and QTSim use different random numbers, so the same input gives a different realisation. On average the Julia model with QTSim’s crossover model reproduces the published values to within about one per cent. Applying every crossover point instead gives populations that hold about 6% more genetic variance and survive about 12% longer than the published ones, so the published results depend on QTSim’s single-crossover recombination (Loci and recombination)."},{"title":"The categorical layer","page":"The categorical layer","location":"tutorials/04_categorical_layer/#The-categorical-layer","category":"section","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nSetup\nThe population schema\nFrom arrays to an ACSet and back\nA generation as a composite of operators\nStratifying with ⊗"},{"title":"Overview","page":"The categorical layer","location":"tutorials/04_categorical_layer/#Overview","category":"section","text":"QTSim runs one generation as a single loop over the population. This package splits that loop into separate operators and keeps two descriptions of the population:\n\nan abstract one, in which the population is an attributed C-set (ACSet), the categorical data structure of AlgebraicJulia (Patterson et al. 2022), and each evolutionary process is an Operator that can be composed with ⋅ and stratified with ⊗;\na lowered one, the array kernel that does the numerical work and reproduces QTSim’s output.\n\nThis vignette shows the population as an ACSet, moves it between the two levels, and builds a generation out of operators. The facts that make the decomposition legitimate, that composition is associative and that a run is the iterate of one composed generation, are proved in Lean in lean/CategoricalQuantGen/Composition.lean."},{"title":"Setup","page":"The categorical layer","location":"tutorials/04_categorical_layer/#Setup","category":"section","text":"The categorical layer is a package extension of QTLSim, which Julia loads as soon as Catlab is loaded. Catlab exports names of its own, such as swap (the symmetric braiding), that would clash with the package’s operators, so we load it with import and bring in only the functions we need. operators() returns the operators of one generation.\n\nusing QTLSim\nimport Catlab                       # loads QTLSim's categorical layer\nusing Catlab: ⋅, ⊗, nparts, incident, subpart, set_subpart!, generators\n(; develop, select, reproduce, mutate, swap, generation) = operators()\nusing Printf, Random"},{"title":"The population schema","page":"The categorical layer","location":"tutorials/04_categorical_layer/#The-population-schema","category":"section","text":"The schema, population_schema(), has two kinds of part, individuals and alleles, and a map owner sending each allele to the individual that carries it. Allele effects and the individuals’ environmental deviation, genotype, phenotype and fitness are attributes.\n\nAllele ──owner──▶ Individual        effect : Allele → ℝ\n                                    env, genotype, phenotype, fitness : Individual → ℝ\n\n(objects = nameof.(generators(population_schema(), :Ob)),\n maps = nameof.(generators(population_schema(), :Hom)),\n attributes = nameof.(generators(population_schema(), :Attr)))\n\n(objects = [:Individual, :Allele], maps = [:owner], attributes = [:effect, :env, :genotype, :phenotype, :fitness])"},{"title":"From arrays to an ACSet and back","page":"The categorical layer","location":"tutorials/04_categorical_layer/#From-arrays-to-an-ACSet-and-back","category":"section","text":"The kernel keeps the population in a World: genomes in a (NoQTL, Ploidy, PopSize) array plus per-individual vectors. lift turns a World into a Population ACSet. For a population small enough to print, with effects rounded so they are easy to read:\n\np = Params(PopSize = 3, Ploidy = 2, NoQTL = 2, PhenotypeVar = 0.02, Heritability = 0.4)\nw = World(p)\nrng = Xoshiro(1)\nw.genome .= round.(0.1 .* randn(rng, size(w.genome)), digits = 3)\nw.env .= round.(sqrt(envVar(p)) .* randn(rng, p.PopSize), digits = 3)\ndevelop!(w)                    # genotype = sum of effects; phenotype = genotype + env\npop = lift(w)\n\n<div class=\"c-set\">\n\nQTLSimCatlabExt.PopulationACSet{Float64, Float64} {Individual:3, Allele:12, Effect:0, Trait:0}\n\nIndividual env genotype phenotype fitness\n1 -0.043 -0.021 -0.064 0.0\n2 0.064 0.056 0.12 0.0\n3 -0.003 0.241 0.238 0.0\n\nAllele owner effect\n1 1 0.006\n2 1 0.028\n3 1 -0.06\n4 1 0.005\n5 2 0.109\n6 2 -0.158\n7 2 0.018\n8 2 0.087\n9 3 0.097\n10 3 0.155\n11 3 -0.058\n12 3 0.047\n\n</div>\n\nEach individual owns Ploidy × NoQTL = 4 alleles. Its genotype is the sum of the effects of the alleles in the preimage of owner, which incident returns. genotypes computes all of them this way, at the abstract level, and they agree with the kernel’s.\n\nalleles = incident(pop, 1, :owner)\n(alleles = alleles, effects = subpart(pop, alleles, :effect),\n genotype_of_individual_1 = sum(subpart(pop, alleles, :effect)),\n all_genotypes_agree = genotypes(pop) ≈ w.genotype)\n\n(alleles = [1, 2, 3, 4], effects = [0.006, 0.028, -0.06, 0.005], genotype_of_individual_1 = -0.020999999999999994, all_genotypes_agree = true)\n\nThe translation goes both ways. A change made to the ACSet, here giving one allele of individual 1 a large effect, reaches the kernel through lower!:\n\nset_subpart!(pop, alleles[1], :effect, 1.0)\nw2 = World(p)\nlower!(pop, w2)\ndevelop!(w2)\n(genotype_before = w.genotype[1], genotype_after = w2.genotype[1],\n others_unchanged = w2.genotype[2:3] == w.genotype[2:3])\n\n(genotype_before = -0.020999999999999994, genotype_after = 0.973, others_unchanged = true)\n\nThe round trip is exact for a full-sized population too:\n\nbig = Params(PopSize = 1000, Ploidy = 2, NoQTL = 100, PhenotypeVar = 0.02, Heritability = 0.4)\nwb = World(big)\nwb.genome .= 0.05 .* randn(Xoshiro(2), size(wb.genome))\ndevelop!(wb)\nt = @elapsed popb = lift(wb)\nwb2 = World(big); lower!(popb, wb2)\n@printf(\"%d alleles; lift took %.2f s; genome recovered exactly: %s\\n\",\n        nparts(popb, :Allele), t, wb2.genome == wb.genome)\n\n200000 alleles; lift took 0.02 s; genome recovered exactly: true"},{"title":"A generation as a composite of operators","page":"The categorical layer","location":"tutorials/04_categorical_layer/#A-generation-as-a-composite-of-operators","category":"section","text":"Each step of the life cycle is an operator: develop computes genotypes and phenotypes, select moves the optimum if it is due to move and draws the survivors, reproduce makes the offspring, record notes the parents’ statistics (here it discards them), rescale changes the width of the fitness function if a change is due, mutate adds mutations to the offspring and swap makes the offspring the next parents. One generation is their composite, written with Catlab’s diagrammatic composition ⋅, which reads left to right:\n\ngeneration\n\nOperator(:develop⋅select⋅reproduce⋅record⋅rescale⋅mutate⋅swap)\n\n(f ⋅ g)(w, rng) applies f and then g, skipping the rest of a composite once the population is extinct. Because composition is associative, how a composite is bracketed does not matter. Lean proves this for composition in general; here it is for one case, run for 50 generations from the same state and seed:\n\nq = Params(PopSize = 200, Ploidy = 2, NoQTL = 20, PhenotypeVar = 0.02, Heritability = 0.4,\n           MutationScalar = 0.004, MutationRatePerGamete = 0.01, RecombPoissonMean = 3.0)\n\nfunction iterate_op(op, q; gens = 50, seed = 1)\n    w = World(q); rng = Xoshiro(seed)\n    w.env .= sqrt(envVar(q)) .* randn(rng, q.PopSize)\n    for _ in 1:gens; op(w, rng); end\n    w\nend\n\nleft = iterate_op((develop ⋅ select) ⋅ (reproduce ⋅ mutate ⋅ swap), q)\nright = iterate_op(develop ⋅ (select ⋅ (reproduce ⋅ (mutate ⋅ swap))), q)\nleft.genome == right.genome\n\ntrue\n\nThe second fact Lean proves (loop_eq_iterate) is that running the generation loop n times is the n-fold iterate of one generation. run_composed drives a run by applying the composite generation, recording statistics as it goes; run! is the hand-written loop of the kernel, in QTSim’s order. Under the same seed they produce the same run, bit for bit:\n\nqr = Params(q; NoGenerations = 300, MoveFitnessSDGen = 150, MoveFitnessSD = 0.01,\n            MoveType = :continuous)\na = run!(World(qr), Xoshiro(7))\nb = run_composed(World(qr), Xoshiro(7))\n(genetic_variance = a.geno_var == b.geno_var, mean_phenotype = a.pheno_mean == b.pheno_mean,\n survivors = a.nsurv == b.nsurv, optimum = a.theta == b.theta)\n\n(genetic_variance = true, mean_phenotype = true, survivors = true, optimum = true)"},{"title":"Stratifying with ⊗","page":"The categorical layer","location":"tutorials/04_categorical_layer/#Stratifying-with","category":"section","text":"The monoidal product ⊗ turns one population’s parameters into a metapopulation of independent demes. An operator built for one deme acts on every deme of a Stratified state, and a migration operator, composed in with ⋅, couples them.\n\nmeta = p ⊗ demes(4)\nrng = Xoshiro(3)\nfor d in meta.demes; d.env .= sqrt(envVar(p)) .* randn(rng, p.PopSize); end\nstep = generation ⋅ migration(0.25)\nstep(meta, rng)\n(demes = ndemes(meta), total = totalpop(meta), generation_of_each_deme = [d.gen for d in meta.demes])\n\n(demes = 4, total = 12, generation_of_each_deme = [1, 1, 1, 1])\n\nRecomposing the model uses these pieces to change the model’s biology without touching the kernel. The operators are themselves the kernel’s image of a life cycle written once as syntax: One life cycle, two backends derives them, and a SLiM model, from it.\n\n<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-Patterson2022\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nPatterson, Evan, Owen Lynch, and James Fairbanks. 2022. “Categorical Data Structures for Technical Computing.” Compositionality 4: 5. https://doi.org/10.32408/compositionality-4-5.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"title":"Adaptation to a moving optimum","page":"Adaptation to a moving optimum","location":"tutorials/03_moving_optimum/#Adaptation-to-a-moving-optimum","category":"section","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nSetup\nThree ways to move the optimum\nAnatomy of an extinction\nThe paper’s measures\nHow long populations last"},{"title":"Overview","page":"Adaptation to a moving optimum","location":"tutorials/03_moving_optimum/#Overview","category":"section","text":"The paper asks how fast an environment can change before a population that adapts to it goes extinct (Pagel et al. 2025). Its experiments move the optimum of the fitness function by a fixed amount every generation and record how the population responds. This vignette looks at the ways the optimum can move, follows one population to extinction, defines the measures the paper uses to summarise a run, and shows how the time to extinction depends on the rate of change. The theory behind the experiment goes back to Lynch and Lande (Lynch and Lande 1993) and Bürger and Lynch (Bürger and Lynch 1995): a population follows a moving optimum with a lag, and the lag it can sustain is limited by its genetic variance and by how strongly selection acts."},{"title":"Setup","page":"Adaptation to a moving optimum","location":"tutorials/03_moving_optimum/#Setup","category":"section","text":"using QTLSim\nusing CairoMakie, Printf, Statistics\nCairoMakie.activate!(type = \"png\")"},{"title":"Three ways to move the optimum","page":"Adaptation to a moving optimum","location":"tutorials/03_moving_optimum/#Three-ways-to-move-the-optimum","category":"section","text":"From generation MoveFitnessSDGen on, the optimum theta is updated at the start of every generation, in one of three ways chosen by MoveType:\n\n:continuous moves it by a fixed amount, theta leftarrow theta + d, with d given by MoveFitnessSD. This is a steadily changing environment, and it is what the paper uses.\n:phenotype resets it to the current mean phenotype plus d phenotypic standard deviations, theta = bar z + dsqrtV_P.\n:fitness resets it to the mean plus d fitness-function widths, theta = bar z + domega.\n\nThe last two keep the optimum a fixed distance ahead of the population, so the lag theta - bar z is set by construction and the population can never fall behind. Only the continuous move lets the lag evolve.\n\np = Params(PopSize = 1000, Ploidy = 2, NoQTL = 100, NoGenerations = 2600,\n           PhenotypeVar = 0.02, Heritability = 0.4, MutationScalar = 0.004,\n           MutationRatePerGamete = 1e-4, RecombPoissonMean = 3.0, MoveFitnessSDGen = 2000)\nruns = [(name, simulate(Params(p; MoveType = type, MoveFitnessSD = d); seed = 1))\n        for (name, type, d) in ((\"continuous, d = 0.005\", :continuous, 0.005),\n                                (\"phenotype, d = 1\", :phenotype, 1.0),\n                                (\"fitness, d = 0.1\", :fitness, 0.1))]\nnothing\n\nfig = Figure(size = (640, 340))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"generation\", ylabel = \"lag θ − z̄\", limits = ((1950, 2600), nothing))\nfor ((name, r), c) in zip(runs, (:crimson, :navy, :teal))\n    lines!(ax, r.gen, r.theta .- r.pheno_mean, color = c, label = name)\nend\naxislegend(ax, position = :rb)\nfig\n\n<div id=\"fig-move-types\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 1: The lag θ − z̄ between the optimum and the mean phenotype after the optimum starts moving at generation 2000. The phenotype and fitness moves hold the lag at √V_P·d and ω·d; under the continuous move the lag grows until the response to selection matches the rate of change.\n\n</div>"},{"title":"Anatomy of an extinction","page":"Adaptation to a moving optimum","location":"tutorials/03_moving_optimum/#Anatomy-of-an-extinction","category":"section","text":"The paper’s populations first spend 10,000 generations under stabilising selection and then face a continuously moving optimum. Here is one such population, with parameters near the middle of the paper’s ranges, followed until it dies out (compare the paper’s Supplementary Figure S3).\n\npaperlike = Params(PopSize = 1000, Ploidy = 2, NoQTL = 100, NoGenerations = 12_000,\n                   PhenotypeVar = 0.022, Heritability = 0.32, MutationScalar = 0.004,\n                   MutationRatePerGamete = 1e-4, RecombPoissonMean = 3.0, InitFitnessSD = 5.0,\n                   MoveFitnessSDGen = 10_000, MoveType = :continuous)\nex = simulate(Params(paperlike; MoveFitnessSD = 0.05); seed = 3)\n(extinct = ex.extinct, extinct_generation = ex.extinct_gen)\n\n(extinct = true, extinct_generation = 10441)\n\nwindow = (9_900, ex.extinct ? ex.extinct_gen + 10 : 12_000)\nfig = Figure(size = (960, 300))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"generation\", ylabel = \"phenotype / optimum\", title = \"(a)\",\n           limits = (window, nothing))\nlines!(ax1, ex.gen, ex.theta, color = :gray, label = \"optimum\")\nlines!(ax1, ex.gen, ex.pheno_mean, color = :navy, label = \"mean phenotype\")\naxislegend(ax1, position = :lt)\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], xlabel = \"generation\", ylabel = \"genetic variance\", title = \"(b)\",\n           limits = (window, nothing))\nlines!(ax2, ex.gen, ex.geno_var, color = :teal)\nax3 = Axis(fig[1, 3], xlabel = \"generation\", ylabel = \"survivors\", title = \"(c)\",\n           limits = (window, (0, 1050)))\nlines!(ax3, ex.gen, ex.nsurv, color = :crimson)\nfor ax in (ax1, ax2, ax3); vlines!(ax, [10_000], color = :black, linestyle = :dash); end\nfig\n\n<div id=\"fig-extinction\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 2: One population after the optimum starts moving at generation 10,000 (dashed line). (a) The mean phenotype falls ever further behind the optimum. (b) Genetic variance is eroded as survival collapses. (c) The number of individuals surviving selection each generation; the population is refilled to 1000 by the survivors’ offspring until fewer than two survive.\n\n</div>"},{"title":"The paper’s measures","page":"Adaptation to a moving optimum","location":"tutorials/03_moving_optimum/#The-paper’s-measures","category":"section","text":"The paper summarises each run by a handful of numbers, all measured in units of the phenotypic standard deviation so that populations with different variances can be compared. With the move starting at generation g_0:\n\nGenerations to extinction T: the number of generations of directional selection until the population dies, counting the generation in which it dies.\n50% mortality: the first generation after the move at which the number of survivors is closest to N2; T_50 counts generations to it in the same way as T. The phenotypic standard deviation s_50 at that generation is the unit for everything else.\nStandardised environmental change x = d  s_50.\nStandardised rate of phenotypic change: how far the mean phenotype moved, per generation, in units of s_50, either until extinction or until 50% mortality.\nStandardised lag: x minus the rate of phenotypic change to extinction, the amount per generation by which the population falls further behind.\n\nThe function below computes them from a run, following the conventions of the paper’s results table, which counts generations from one (so its “generation 10,000” is the last generation before the move). The replication vignette checks it against the table itself.\n\nfunction paper_measures(o, p::Params)\n    g0, N, d = p.MoveFitnessSDGen, p.PopSize, p.MoveFitnessSD\n    sd = sqrt.(o.pheno_var)\n    i0 = findfirst(==(g0 - 1), o.gen)                         # last generation before the move\n    post = findall(>=(g0), o.gen)\n    k = isempty(post) ? lastindex(o.gen) : post[argmin(abs.(o.nsurv[post] .- N ÷ 2))]\n    T = o.extinct ? o.gen[end] + 2 - g0 : length(post)        # the last record precedes the dying generation\n    T50 = o.gen[k] + 2 - g0\n    x = d / sd[k]\n    rate = (o.pheno_mean[end] - o.pheno_mean[i0]) / T / sd[k]\n    rate50 = (o.pheno_mean[k] - o.pheno_mean[i0]) / T50 / sd[k]\n    (; T, survived = !o.extinct, T50, sd50 = sd[k], x, rate, rate50, lag = x - rate)\nend\n\npaper_measures(ex, Params(paperlike; MoveFitnessSD = 0.05))\n\n(T = 442, survived = false, T50 = 154, sd50 = 0.28499912426570245, x = 0.17543913557216897, rate = 0.04213412512639793, rate50 = 0.03911414122609284, lag = 0.13330501044577103)"},{"title":"How long populations last","page":"Adaptation to a moving optimum","location":"tutorials/03_moving_optimum/#How-long-populations-last","category":"section","text":"Now the same population faces a range of rates, four replicates each, each run allowed up to 2000 generations of directional selection. Runs are independent, so they are spread over threads.\n\nrates = [0.002, 0.005, 0.01, 0.015, 0.02, 0.03, 0.05, 0.1, 0.2]\njobs = [(d, rep) for d in rates for rep in 1:4]\nres = Vector{Any}(undef, length(jobs))\nThreads.@threads for k in eachindex(jobs)\n    d, rep = jobs[k]\n    q = Params(paperlike; MoveFitnessSD = d)\n    res[k] = (; d, paper_measures(simulate(q; seed = 100k + rep), q)...)\nend\nfor d in rates\n    rs = filter(r -> r.d == d, res)\n    @printf(\"d = %.3f   x ≈ %.3f   survived %d of 4   median T = %s\\n\", d,\n            median(r.x for r in rs), count(r -> r.survived, rs),\n            all(r -> r.survived, rs) ? \"> 2000\" : string(round(Int, median(r.T for r in rs))))\nend\n\nd = 0.002   x ≈ 0.006   survived 4 of 4   median T = > 2000\nd = 0.005   x ≈ 0.016   survived 4 of 4   median T = > 2000\nd = 0.010   x ≈ 0.036   survived 4 of 4   median T = > 2000\nd = 0.015   x ≈ 0.064   survived 4 of 4   median T = > 2000\nd = 0.020   x ≈ 0.071   survived 0 of 4   median T = 1481\nd = 0.030   x ≈ 0.107   survived 0 of 4   median T = 798\nd = 0.050   x ≈ 0.167   survived 0 of 4   median T = 440\nd = 0.100   x ≈ 0.316   survived 0 of 4   median T = 192\nd = 0.200   x ≈ 0.564   survived 0 of 4   median T = 98\n\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 360))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], xscale = log10, yscale = log10, xlabel = \"standardised rate x = d / s₅₀\",\n           ylabel = \"generations to extinction\")\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], xscale = log10, xlabel = \"standardised rate x = d / s₅₀\",\n           ylabel = \"standardised lag\")\ndead = filter(r -> !r.survived, res); alive = filter(r -> r.survived, res)\nscatter!(ax1, [r.x for r in dead], [r.T for r in dead], color = :crimson)\nscatter!(ax1, [r.x for r in alive], [r.T for r in alive], color = :white,\n         strokecolor = :teal, strokewidth = 1.5)\nscatter!(ax2, [r.x for r in res], [r.lag for r in res],\n         color = [r.survived ? :teal : :crimson for r in res])\nfig\n\n<div id=\"fig-rate-sweep\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 3: Generations to extinction (left) and standardised lag (right) against the standardised rate of environmental change x for one parameter set. Open circles (left) and teal points (right) are populations still alive after 2000 generations of directional selection; the others died.\n\n</div>\n\nPopulations that survive keep pace: their lag per generation is close to zero. Once the environment moves faster than they can respond, the lag grows every generation and the population dies within a few hundred generations, sooner the faster the change. For this parameter set every population survived 2000 generations at rates up to about 6% of a phenotypic standard deviation per generation, and every one died at about 7% and above. Surviving 2000 generations is not surviving indefinitely, though: the paper ran its populations for up to 240,000 generations, and its estimate of the sustainable rate, about 3% of a standard deviation per generation, comes from the populations that lasted that long. The replication vignette maps the boundary across the paper’s whole parameter space.\n\n<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-BurgerLynch1995\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nBürger, Reinhard, and Michael Lynch. 1995. “Evolution and Extinction in a Changing Environment: A Quantitative-Genetic Analysis.” Evolution 49 (1): 151–63. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1558-5646.1995.tb05967.x.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-LynchLande1993\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nLynch, Michael, and Russell Lande. 1993. “Evolution and Extinction in Response to Environmental Change.” In Biotic Interactions and Global Change, edited by Peter M. Kareiva, Joel G. Kingsolver, and Raymond B. Huey. Sinauer.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Pagel2025\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nPagel, Mark, Jacob D. Gardner, and Andrew Meade. 2025. “Quantitative-Genetic Analysis of Directional Adaptation Suggests Low Maximum Sustainable Rates of Change in Agreement with Data from Field Populations.” Scientific Reports 15. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-24445-2.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"title":"QTLSim.jl","page":"Home","location":"#QTLSim.jl","category":"section","text":"QTLSim.jl is a Julia port of QTSim, the simulation behind Pagel, Gardner and Meade, Quantitative-genetic analysis of directional adaptation suggests low maximum sustainable rates of change in agreement with data from field populations (Scientific Reports, 2025). QTSim is a C program by Andrew Meade (University of Reading); its source calls it QTLSim 1.0.\n\nThe model follows a population of diploid individuals with one additive quantitative trait, under Gaussian stabilising selection on viability toward an optimum that can move. Survivors mate at random, gametes recombine at Poisson crossover points, and offspring receive Poisson numbers of Gaussian mutations. The paper used it to estimate how fast a population can track a steadily changing environment before it goes extinct: about 3% of a phenotypic standard deviation per generation."},{"title":"Installation","page":"Home","location":"#Installation","category":"section","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/ecorecipes/QTLSim.jl\")"},{"title":"Quick start","page":"Home","location":"#Quick-start","category":"section","text":"A population at mutation–selection balance whose optimum then moves by a fixed amount every generation:\n\nusing QTLSim\n\np = Params(PopSize = 1000, Ploidy = 2, NoQTL = 100, NoGenerations = 12_000,\n           PhenotypeVar = 0.022, Heritability = 0.32, MutationScalar = 0.004,\n           MutationRatePerGamete = 1e-4, RecombPoissonMean = 3.0, InitFitnessSD = 5.0,\n           MoveFitnessSDGen = 10_000, MoveFitnessSD = 0.05, MoveType = :continuous)\nout = simulate(p; seed = 1)\nout.extinct, out.extinct_gen\n\nParams takes QTSim's options under the same names, and simulate returns the mean and variance of the phenotype, the genetic variance, the number of survivors and the optimum for every sampled generation."},{"title":"The categorical layer","page":"Home","location":"#The-categorical-layer","category":"section","text":"Loading Catlab adds a categorical description of the same model, as the package extension QTLSimCatlabExt. The life cycle is written once, as a morphism of a free category whose objects are the stages of a generation and whose generators are its steps, and functors lower it to the simulation kernel and to a model for the population-genetics simulator SLiM, in its language Eidos:\n\nimport Catlab\nusing Catlab: ⋅, ⊗\nusing Random\nL = lifecycle()\nqtsim = L.develop ⋅ L.select ⋅ L.reproduce ⋅ L.record ⋅ L.rescale ⋅ L.mutate ⋅ L.swap\n# develop⋅select⋅reproduce⋅record⋅rescale⋅mutate⋅swap: Zygotes → Zygotes\n\nrun_composed(World(p), Xoshiro(1); cycle = qtsim)   # the same run as run!, bit for bit\nprint(slim_script(qtsim, Params(p; NoQTL = 1)))     # the same life cycle as a SLiM model\n\nThe SLiM model generated this way agrees with QTSim in distribution. In the kernel the steps are operators, which can be rearranged, extended or stratified into demes, and the population is also an attributed C-set (ACSet):\n\n(; develop, select, reproduce, mutate, swap, generation) = operators()\ndouble = develop ⋅ select ⋅ reproduce ⋅ mutate ⋅ mutate ⋅ swap   # twice the mutation\nmeta = p ⊗ demes(4)                                               # four demes\n(generation ⋅ migration(0.01))(meta, Xoshiro(1))                  # with migration"},{"title":"Validation","page":"Home","location":"#Validation","category":"section","text":"The kernel reproduces QTSim at three strengths:\n\nStatistically: run many times, the Julia model and QTSim give the same distributions of genetic variance, survival and adaptation.\nExactly: a replica that draws its random numbers from QTSim's GSL routines in QTSim's order matches QTSim's output generation by generation.\nAgainst the published results: on 589 of the paper's input files, the Julia model's genetic variance before the move and its time to extinction are within about 1% of the published values.\n\nThe validation tutorial shows all three, and the replication reproduces the paper's results."},{"title":"Tutorials","page":"Home","location":"#Tutorials","category":"section","text":"Getting started\nMutation–selection balance\nAdaptation to a moving optimum\nThe categorical layer\nRecomposing the model\nOne life cycle, two backends\nLoci and recombination\nValidation against QTSim\nReplicating Pagel, Gardner and Meade (2025)"},{"title":"Licence","page":"Home","location":"#Licence","category":"section","text":"QTLSim.jl is free software under the GNU General Public License, version 3 or (at your option) any later version. It reimplements the algorithm of QTSim (copyright 2023 Andrew Meade, University of Reading), which is distributed under the same licence."},{"title":"Replicating Pagel, Gardner and Meade (2025)","page":"Replicating Pagel, Gardner and Meade (2025)","location":"tutorials/09_replicating_pagel_2025/#Replicating-Pagel,-Gardner-and-Meade-(2025)","category":"section","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nSetup\nThe paper’s results, recomputed\nRerunning a sample with QTLSim\nPopulation by population\nTable 2\nFigure 1\nGenetic variance at equilibrium\nHow fast is too fast\nSurvivors\nSummary"},{"title":"Overview","page":"Replicating Pagel, Gardner and Meade (2025)","location":"tutorials/09_replicating_pagel_2025/#Overview","category":"section","text":"Pagel, Gardner and Meade (Pagel et al. 2025) simulated 119,984 populations with QTSim, in eight strata of about 15,000: 100, 500, 1000 or 5000 loci, with a mean of three or five crossover points per gamete. Each population had 1000 diploid individuals and its own phenotypic variance, heritability, mutation scalar, fitness width and rate of environmental change, drawn from the priors in the paper’s Table 1. It spent 10,000 generations under stabilising selection; then its optimum moved by a fixed amount every generation until it went extinct or 240,000 generations had passed. From these runs the paper reports how long populations survive and how fast they change (its Table 2), how survival, lag and mortality depend on the rate of environmental change (its Figure 1), a maximum sustainable rate of about 3% of a phenotypic standard deviation per generation, and a comparison with rates measured in the field.\n\nThis vignette replicates those results in two steps. It first recomputes the paper’s numbers from the paper’s own per-run results table, which pins down exactly how each is defined. It then reruns a stratified random sample of 710 of the paper’s populations with QTLSim, measures them in the same way, and compares them with the published runs of the same populations and with the paper’s full results.\n\nThree things differ from the paper. Each rerun stops 2000 generations after the optimum starts to move, rather than 240,000, unless the population is still alive then: most populations are dead by then, and comparisons of the 2000-generation reruns with the published runs are made at the same horizon. The populations still alive are run on to 240,000 generations for the paper’s statistics of the survivors. The sample takes 125 populations from each stratum with 100 or 500 loci, 75 with 1000 and 30 with 5000, where runs are slowest, and weights every stratum equally when pooling, as the paper’s equal-sized strata do. And the reruns use QTLSim’s own random numbers, so each one is a new realisation of the same population, not a copy of the published run.\n\nThe results tables come with the paper’s code and data, not with this package. The vignette looks for them in a Repository/Results folder next to the package’s folder."},{"title":"Setup","page":"Replicating Pagel, Gardner and Meade (2025)","location":"tutorials/09_replicating_pagel_2025/#Setup","category":"section","text":"using QTLSim\nusing CairoMakie, DelimitedFiles, Distributions, Printf, Random, Statistics, XLSX\nCairoMakie.activate!(type = \"png\")\n\nconst PKG = normpath(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\"))\nconst RESULTS = normpath(joinpath(PKG, \"..\", \"Repository\", \"Results\"))\nconst MASTER = joinpath(RESULTS, \"Master Output file.xlsx\")\nconst FIGDATA = joinpath(RESULTS, \"Figures 1-2 and SI Figures 1-3 data.xlsx\")\nconst CACHE = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"replication_runs.csv\")\nconst SURVIVOR_CACHE = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"survivor_runs.csv\")\nisfile(MASTER) || error(\"this vignette needs the paper's results tables in $RESULTS\")\nThreads.nthreads()\n\n6"},{"title":"The paper’s results, recomputed","page":"Replicating Pagel, Gardner and Meade (2025)","location":"tutorials/09_replicating_pagel_2025/#The-paper’s-results,-recomputed","category":"section","text":"The paper’s results table has one row per population: its parameters and the measures computed from its run. We read the columns used here.\n\nconst MOVE_GEN = 10_000      # generations of stabilising selection before the optimum starts to move\nconst CAP = 2_000            # generations of directional selection simulated (the paper ran up to 240 000)\nconst POP = 1000\n# populations rerun from each stratum (each locus count and recombination rate): fewer\n# where runs are slow; pooled statistics weight every stratum equally, as in the paper\nconst SAMPLE_SIZE = Dict(100 => 125, 500 => 125, 1000 => 75, 5000 => 30)\n\n\"Read the columns used here from the paper's per-run results table.\"\nfunction read_master(path)\n    dt = XLSX.readtable(path, \"Master output file\")\n    lab = strip.(string.(dt.column_labels))\n    num(v) = v isa AbstractString ? parse(Float64, v) : v === missing ? NaN : Float64(v)\n    f(name) = num.(dt.data[findfirst(==(name), lab)])\n    (; L = round.(Int, f(\"No. Loci\")), u = f(\"recombination u=3,5\"), h2 = f(\"Heritability\"),\n       VP = f(\"PhenotypeVar\"), m = f(\"MutationScalar Vm/Ve\"), d = f(\"d_env\"), ω = f(\"w, fitness function sd\"),\n       T = f(\"Generations to extinction\"), T50 = f(\"Generations to 50% mortality\"),\n       sd50 = f(\"Phenotype SD at 50% mortality\"), x = f(\"d_env/trait sd at 50% mortality\"),\n       rate_ext = f(\"Standardised phenotypic change to extinction\"),\n       rate50 = f(\"standardised phenotypic change to 50% mortality\"), lag = f(\"standardised lag\"),\n       P47 = f(\"P Survived at generation 10047\"), VG = f(\"Genotype Var at 10000\"),\n       shc = f(\"Burger Vg(SHC) eq 21a\"), kc = f(\"standardised kc\"),\nadjusted = f(\"mutation adjusted standardised change\"), survived = f(\"Extinct (0,1)\") .== 1)\nend\n\nt = read_master(MASTER)\n@printf(\"%d populations, %d of which survived all 240,000 generations\\n\", length(t.L), count(t.survived))\n\n119984 populations, 6158 of which survived all 240,000 generations\n\nThe paper’s Table 2 summarises the populations by medians and interquartile ranges. The rate of phenotypic change is how far the mean phenotype moved per generation, until extinction or until 50% mortality, in units of the phenotypic standard deviation at 50% mortality; Adaptation to a moving optimum defines each measure. The quantiles below are weighted, which matters for the sample later; for the full table every weight is one.\n\nfunction wquantile(v, w, p)\n    o = sortperm(v); c = cumsum(w[o])\n    v[o[searchsortedfirst(c, p * c[end])]]\nend\nfunction iqr(v, w = ones(length(v)))\n    ok = isfinite.(v)\n    @sprintf(\"%.3g (%.3g–%.3g)\", (wquantile(v[ok], w[ok], p) for p in (0.5, 0.25, 0.75))...)\nend\nprintln(\"| quantity | paper | recomputed from the table |\\n|:--|:--|:--|\")\nfor (name, paper, v) in (\n        (\"generations to extinction, all populations\", \"141 (79–326)\", t.T),\n        (\"generations to 50% mortality, all populations\", \"47 (25–113)\", t.T50),\n        (\"rate of phenotypic change to extinction, all populations\", \"0.056 (0.030–0.103)\", t.rate_ext),\n        (\"rate of phenotypic change to 50% mortality, all populations\", \"0.033 (0.017–0.058)\", t.rate50),\n        (\"rate of phenotypic change, survivors\", \"0.031 (0.011–0.066)\", t.rate_ext[t.survived]),\n        (\"critical rate k_c, survivors\", \"0.022 (0.008–0.056)\", t.kc[t.survived]))\n    println(\"| \", name, \" | \", paper, \" | \", iqr(v), \" |\")\nend\n\nquantity paper recomputed from the table\ngenerations to extinction, all populations 141 (79–326) 141 (79–326)\ngenerations to 50% mortality, all populations 47 (25–113) 47 (25–113)\nrate of phenotypic change to extinction, all populations 0.056 (0.030–0.103) 0.0564 (0.0302–0.103)\nrate of phenotypic change to 50% mortality, all populations 0.033 (0.017–0.058) 0.0327 (0.017–0.058)\nrate of phenotypic change, survivors 0.031 (0.011–0.066) 0.0307 (0.0117–0.0661)\ncritical rate k_c, survivors 0.022 (0.008–0.056) 0.0222 (0.00821–0.0559)\n\nThe survivors’ median rate of phenotypic change, about 0.03 standard deviations per generation, is the paper’s estimate of the maximum sustainable rate. The survivors’ mutation scalars are larger than those of the populations as a whole, so the paper also reweights them (its equation 1): each survivor’s rate is multiplied by the density of its mutation scalar under a gamma distribution, relative to the survivors’ mean density. The gamma distribution turns out to be the maximum-likelihood fit to the mutation scalars of all 119,984 populations, and with it the adjustment reproduces the table’s column:\n\nconst MGAMMA = fit_mle(Gamma, t.m)\n\"Rates weighted by the density of their mutation scalars, relative to the (weighted) mean density.\"\nmutation_adjusted(rate, m, w = ones(length(m))) =\n    rate .* pdf.(MGAMMA, m) ./ (sum(w .* pdf.(MGAMMA, m)) / sum(w))\nadj = mutation_adjusted(t.rate_ext[t.survived], t.m[t.survived])\n(gamma_shape = shape(MGAMMA), gamma_scale = scale(MGAMMA),\n largest_relative_difference = maximum(abs.(adj ./ t.adjusted[t.survived] .- 1)),\n median_adjusted = median(adj))\n\n(gamma_shape = 1.2010380894230446, gamma_scale = 0.004635457830592143, largest_relative_difference = 6.544547792586286e-9, median_adjusted = 0.019677242570054217)\n\nThe adjusted median, 0.0197, is the paper’s 0.019.\n\nFigure 1d of the paper fits the time to extinction against the standardised lag, the amount per generation by which a population falls further behind the optimum, with a quadratic in natural logarithms. It reports log T = 40 - 091 log(textlag) + dots with R^2 = 083:\n\nfunction logfit(lag, T, w = ones(length(T)))\n    ok = (lag .> 0) .& (T .> 0) .& isfinite.(lag)\n    x = log.(lag[ok]); y = log.(T[ok]); s = sqrt.(w[ok])\n    X = [ones(length(x)) x x .^ 2]\n    b = (s .* X) \\ (s .* y)\n    ȳ = sum(w[ok] .* y) / sum(w[ok])\n    (; intercept = b[1], slope = b[2], quadratic = b[3],\n       R2 = 1 - sum(w[ok] .* (y - X * b) .^ 2) / sum(w[ok] .* (y .- ȳ) .^ 2), n = count(ok))\nend\nlogfit(t.lag, t.T)\n\n(intercept = 3.9991256253251874, slope = -0.9127023301283174, quadratic = -0.018345962516546244, R2 = 0.833178298162401, n = 119801)\n\nThe field comparison works with the critical rate k_c of the surviving populations, the fastest change theory says each could sustain (Lynch and Lande 1993; Bürger and Lynch 1995). Birds advance their breeding by a median of 0.022 phenotypic standard deviations a year; with a generation time of two to four years that is 0.044 to 0.088 per generation, which the paper places at the 68th and 86th percentiles of k_c:\n\nkc = filter(isfinite, t.kc[t.survived])\nfor rate in (0.044, 0.088)\n    @printf(\"%.3f standard deviations per generation is at the %.1fth percentile of k_c (%d survivors)\\n\",\n            rate, 100 * mean(kc .<= rate), length(kc))\nend\n\n0.044 standard deviations per generation is at the 68.3th percentile of k_c (5755 survivors)\n0.088 standard deviations per generation is at the 86.2th percentile of k_c (5755 survivors)\n\nThe critical rate is the paper’s equation 2, Bürger and Lynch’s maximum rate of environmental change a population can sustain. It needs only quantities that every run reports: the genetic variance sigma_g^2 just before the move, the fitness width omega, the environmental variance V_E, and the number n of survivors at the table’s generation 10,047, from which the table takes the expected number of offspring per surviving parent N_0 = N(2n-3)(2n(n-2)), the variance in offspring number V_k = 2N_0(1 - 2N_0N) and Falconer’s effective size N_e = 4N(V_k + 2). With V_s = omega^2 + V_E, V_lambda = V_s + sigma_g^2 + V_s(2N_e) and B_0 = N_0omegasqrtV_lambda, the table’s column is\n\nk_c = fracsigma_g^2sigma_g^2 + V_ssqrt2 V_lambda ln B_0\n\nin units of the phenotypic standard deviation at 50% mortality, where B_0  1. The equation as printed has no factor of 2 and has V_s + sigma_g^2 in place of V_lambda; the table’s form, which gives the paper’s numbers, is Bürger and Lynch’s (Bürger and Lynch 1995). This function reproduces the column:\n\n# Bürger and Lynch's critical rate k_c, as the paper's results table computes it, in units of\n# the phenotypic standard deviation `sd50` at 50% mortality; `n` is the number of survivors\n# at the table's generation 10,047 and `VG` the genetic variance just before the move.\nfunction critical_rate(; n, VG, ω, VE, sd50, N = POP)\n    n >= 3 || return NaN\n    No = N * (2n - 3) / (2n * (n - 2))          # offspring per surviving parent\n    Vk = 2No * (1 - 2No / N)                    # variance in offspring number\n    Ne = 4N / (Vk + 2)                          # Falconer's effective size\n    Vs = ω^2 + VE\n    Vλ = Vs + VG + Vs / (2Ne)\n    B0 = No * ω / sqrt(Vλ)\n    B0 > 1 ? VG / (VG + Vs) * sqrt(2Vλ * log(B0)) / sd50 : NaN\nend\nkc_recomputed = [critical_rate(n = t.P47[i], VG = t.VG[i], ω = t.ω[i], VE = (1 - t.h2[i]) * t.VP[i],\n                               sd50 = t.sd50[i]) for i in eachindex(t.L)]\nok = isfinite.(t.kc)\n(defined_where_the_table_is = isfinite.(kc_recomputed) == ok,\n largest_relative_difference = maximum(abs.(kc_recomputed[ok] ./ t.kc[ok] .- 1)))\n\n(defined_where_the_table_is = true, largest_relative_difference = 8.111256111220655e-11)"},{"title":"Rerunning a sample with QTLSim","page":"Replicating Pagel, Gardner and Meade (2025)","location":"tutorials/09_replicating_pagel_2025/#Rerunning-a-sample-with-QTLSim","category":"section","text":"Each sampled population is simulated with its published parameters: 10,000 generations of stabilising selection, then the optimum moving by its rate d per generation for up to 2000 generations.\n\n\"Sample `n[L]` inputs from each stratum, reproducibly.\"\nfunction stratified_sample(t; n = SAMPLE_SIZE, seed = 2025)\n    rng = Xoshiro(seed)\n    rows = Int[]\n    for L in (100, 500, 1000, 5000), u in (3.0, 5.0)\n        s = findall(i -> t.L[i] == L && t.u[i] == u, eachindex(t.L))\n        append!(rows, sort(shuffle(rng, s)[1:n[L]]))\n    end\n    rows\nend\n\nrows = stratified_sample(t)\nlength(rows)\n\n710\n\nThe measures follow the conventions of the paper’s results table, which numbers generations from one: its “generation 10,000” is the last generation before the move, and its “generation 10,047”, where it records mortality, is the 45th generation of the move. Applied to QTSim’s own output for published populations, this function reproduces the table’s values exactly.\n\n\"\"\"\nThe paper's per-run measures, with the conventions of its results table: generations\nare counted as in the table (the table's 'at 10000' is generation 9999, and its\n'generation 10047' is generation 10044), and 50% mortality is the first generation\nafter the move at which the number of survivors is closest to N/2.\n\"\"\"\nfunction paper_measures(gen, zbar, sd, ns; d, extinct::Bool, movegen = MOVE_GEN, N = POP)\n    i0 = findfirst(==(movegen - 1), gen)\n    post = findall(>=(movegen), gen)\n    k = isempty(post) ? lastindex(gen) : post[argmin(abs.(ns[post] .- N ÷ 2))]\n    T = extinct ? gen[end] + 2 - movegen : length(post)\n    T50 = gen[k] + 2 - movegen\n    sd50 = sd[k]\n    x = d / sd50\n    rate_ext = (zbar[end] - zbar[i0]) / T / sd50\n    rate50 = (zbar[k] - zbar[i0]) / T50 / sd50\n    j47 = findfirst(==(movegen + 44), gen)\n    (; T, extinct, T50, sd50, x, rate_ext, rate50, lag = x - rate_ext,\n       P47 = j47 === nothing ? NaN : Float64(ns[j47]))\nend\n\n\"Simulate one published input with QTLSim and measure it.\"\nfunction replicate_run(t, i; cap = CAP)\n    p = Params(NoGenerations = MOVE_GEN + cap, Sample = 1, PopSize = POP, Ploidy = 2, NoQTL = t.L[i],\n               PhenotypeVar = t.VP[i], Heritability = t.h2[i], MutationScalar = t.m[i],\n               MutationRatePerGamete = 1e-4, RecombPoissonMean = t.u[i], InitFitnessSD = t.ω[i],\n               MoveFitnessSDGen = MOVE_GEN, MoveFitnessSD = t.d[i], MoveType = :continuous)\n    o = simulate(p; seed = i)\n    j = findfirst(==(MOVE_GEN - 1), o.gen)\n    j === nothing && return nothing                       # died before the optimum moved\n    r = paper_measures(o.gen, o.pheno_mean, sqrt.(o.pheno_var), o.nsurv; d = t.d[i], extinct = o.extinct)\n    (; row = i, L = t.L[i], u = t.u[i], r..., VG = o.geno_var[j], ns_eq = mean(o.nsurv[j-99:j]))\nend\n\nconst FIELDS = (:row, :L, :u, :T, :extinct, :T50, :sd50, :x, :rate_ext, :rate50, :lag, :P47, :VG, :ns_eq)\n\nfunction read_runs(path)\n    a, h = readdlm(path, ',', header = true)\n    c(f) = a[:, findfirst(==(string(f)), vec(h))]\n    (; row = Int.(c(:row)), L = Int.(c(:L)), u = Float64.(c(:u)), T = Int.(c(:T)), extinct = Bool.(c(:extinct)),\n       T50 = Int.(c(:T50)), sd50 = Float64.(c(:sd50)), x = Float64.(c(:x)), rate_ext = Float64.(c(:rate_ext)),\n       rate50 = Float64.(c(:rate50)), lag = Float64.(c(:lag)), P47 = Float64.(c(:P47)), VG = Float64.(c(:VG)),\n       ns_eq = Float64.(c(:ns_eq)))\nend\n\n\"\"\"\nRun the sampled inputs on all threads, handing out runs one at a time, longest first.\nEach finished run is appended to `cache`, and runs already there are skipped, so an\ninterrupted replication resumes where it stopped.\n\"\"\"\nfunction run_replication(t, rows, cache; cap = CAP)\n    isfile(cache) || open(io -> println(io, join(FIELDS, ',')), cache, \"w\")\n    done = countlines(cache) > 1 ? Set(read_runs(cache).row) : Set{Int}()\n    todo = filter(∉(done), rows)\n    lk = ReentrantLock()\n    Threads.@threads :greedy for i in todo[sortperm(t.L[todo]; rev = true)]\n        r = replicate_run(t, i; cap)\n        r === nothing && continue\n        lock(lk) do\n            open(io -> println(io, join((getfield(r, f) for f in FIELDS), ',')), cache, \"a\")\n        end\n    end\n    read_runs(cache)\nend\nnothing\n\nThe runs take about an hour on ten threads, mostly for the populations with many loci, so each finished run is stored next to this vignette and read back on later renders. Deleting the file reruns them, and an interrupted replication resumes where it stopped.\n\nruns = run_replication(t, rows, CACHE)\nw = [1 / SAMPLE_SIZE[L] for L in runs.L]                 # every stratum counts equally\n@printf(\"%d runs; they are exactly the sampled populations: %s\\n\",\n        length(runs.row), sort(runs.row) == sort(rows))\n\n710 runs; they are exactly the sampled populations: true\n\nThe stored results come from the code above: rerunning two of the populations reproduces them exactly.\n\nk = findall(==(100), runs.L)[1:2]\ncheck = [replicate_run(t, runs.row[j]) for j in k]\nall(getfield(check[a], f) == getfield(runs, f)[k[a]] for a in 1:2\n    for f in (:T, :T50, :sd50, :x, :rate_ext, :rate50, :lag, :VG, :ns_eq))\n\ntrue"},{"title":"Population by population","page":"Replicating Pagel, Gardner and Meade (2025)","location":"tutorials/09_replicating_pagel_2025/#Population-by-population","category":"section","text":"For each sampled population there are two realisations, the published run and the QTLSim rerun. Their genetic variance just before the optimum moves, and how long they last after it does, should agree on average.\n\npub(f) = getfield(t, f)[runs.row]\ncapT(T) = min.(Float64.(T), CAP)\nprintln(\"| quantity | QTLSim / published | 95% interval |\\n|:--|--:|--:|\")\nfor (name, a, b) in ((\"genetic variance before the move\", runs.VG, pub(:VG)),\n                     (\"generations to extinction, capped at 2000\", capT(runs.T), capT(pub(:T))))\n    ok = (a .> 0) .& (b .> 0)\n    δ = log.(a[ok]) .- log.(b[ok]); ω = w[ok] ./ sum(w[ok])\n    m = sum(ω .* δ); se = sqrt(sum(ω .^ 2 .* (δ .- m) .^ 2))\n    @printf(\"| %s | %.3f | %.3f–%.3f |\\n\", name, exp(m), exp(m - 1.96se), exp(m + 1.96se))\nend\n@printf(\"\\nAlive 2000 generations after the move: QTLSim %.1f%%, published runs %.1f%%.\\n\",\n        100 * sum(w .* .!runs.extinct) / sum(w), 100 * sum(w .* (pub(:T) .>= CAP)) / sum(w))\n\nquantity QTLSim / published 95% interval\ngenetic variance before the move 0.990 0.973–1.007\ngenerations to extinction, capped at 2000 0.999 0.992–1.006\n\nAlive 2000 generations after the move: QTLSim 8.8%, published runs 9.0%."},{"title":"Table 2","page":"Replicating Pagel, Gardner and Meade (2025)","location":"tutorials/09_replicating_pagel_2025/#Table-2","category":"section","text":"The medians and quartiles of Table 2, three ways: for all 119,984 published populations, for the published runs of the sampled populations, and for their QTLSim reruns. For the two samples, times are capped at 2000 generations, and rates for populations still alive then are measured over those 2000 generations.\n\nprintln(\"| quantity | all published | published sample | QTLSim sample |\\n|:--|:--|:--|:--|\")\nfor (name, all_pub, s_pub, s_jl) in (\n        (\"generations to extinction\", t.T, capT(pub(:T)), capT(runs.T)),\n        (\"generations to 50% mortality\", t.T50, pub(:T50), Float64.(runs.T50)),\n        (\"rate of phenotypic change to extinction\", t.rate_ext, pub(:rate_ext), runs.rate_ext),\n        (\"rate of phenotypic change to 50% mortality\", t.rate50, pub(:rate50), runs.rate50),\n        (\"standardised rate of environmental change x\", t.x, pub(:x), runs.x),\n        (\"standardised lag\", t.lag, pub(:lag), runs.lag))\n    println(\"| \", name, \" | \", iqr(all_pub), \" | \", iqr(s_pub, w), \" | \", iqr(s_jl, w), \" |\")\nend\n\nquantity all published published sample QTLSim sample\ngenerations to extinction 141 (79–326) 138 (73–326) 137 (72–334)\ngenerations to 50% mortality 47 (25–113) 45 (22–112) 45 (22–113)\nrate of phenotypic change to extinction 0.0564 (0.0302–0.103) 0.0564 (0.0302–0.105) 0.0583 (0.0273–0.108)\nrate of phenotypic change to 50% mortality 0.0327 (0.017–0.058) 0.0333 (0.0164–0.063) 0.033 (0.0149–0.0637)\nstandardised rate of environmental change x 0.453 (0.231–0.775) 0.485 (0.227–0.814) 0.496 (0.237–0.815)\nstandardised lag 0.349 (0.142–0.671) 0.37 (0.14–0.675) 0.372 (0.144–0.69)"},{"title":"Figure 1","page":"Replicating Pagel, Gardner and Meade (2025)","location":"tutorials/09_replicating_pagel_2025/#Figure-1","category":"section","text":"The paper’s Figure 1 bins populations by the standardised rate of environmental change x, the rate d in units of the phenotypic standard deviation at 50% mortality, and plots medians per bin. Here the paper’s own binned values, from its figure data, are drawn with the same summaries of the two samples in 24 bins of equal weight.\n\nfunction binned(x, y, w = ones(length(x)); nbins = 24)\n    o = sortperm(x); c = cumsum(w[o]) ./ sum(w)\n    bin = min.(max.(ceil.(Int, c .* nbins), 1), nbins)\n    [(wquantile(x[o][bin .== b], w[o][bin .== b], 0.5), wquantile(y[o][bin .== b], w[o][bin .== b], 0.5))\n     for b in 1:nbins if any(==(b), bin)]\nend\nmortality(P47) = replace(1 .- P47 ./ POP, NaN => 1.0)   # dead before generation 47: all died\n\nfig1a = XLSX.readtable(FIGDATA, \"Figure 1a,d\")\npaper1a = sort(unique(collect(zip(Float64.(fig1a.data[1]), Float64.(fig1a.data[3])))))\nfig1bc = XLSX.readtable(FIGDATA, \"Figures 1b,c\")\npaper1bc = [Float64.(c) for c in fig1bc.data]            # x, lag, mortality: 50 bins\n\nsamples = ((pub(:x), capT(pub(:T)), pub(:lag), mortality(pub(:P47)), \"published runs\", :navy),\n           (runs.x, capT(runs.T), runs.lag, mortality(runs.P47), \"QTLSim\", :crimson))\nnothing\n\nfig = Figure(size = (1000, 800))\nax_a = Axis(fig[1, 1], xscale = log10, yscale = log10, title = \"(a)\",\n            xlabel = \"standardised rate of environmental change x\", ylabel = \"generations to extinction\")\nax_b = Axis(fig[1, 2], xscale = log10, yscale = log10, title = \"(b)\",\n            xlabel = \"standardised rate of environmental change x\", ylabel = \"standardised lag\")\nax_c = Axis(fig[2, 1], xscale = log10, title = \"(c)\", xlabel = \"standardised lag\",\n            ylabel = \"mortality at generation 47\")\nax_d = Axis(fig[2, 2], xscale = log10, yscale = log10, title = \"(d)\", xlabel = \"standardised lag\",\n            ylabel = \"generations to extinction\")\nscatterlines!(ax_a, first.(paper1a), min.(last.(paper1a), CAP), color = :gray, label = \"paper\")\nok = paper1bc[2] .> 0\nscatterlines!(ax_b, paper1bc[1][ok], paper1bc[2][ok], color = :gray)\nscatterlines!(ax_c, paper1bc[2][ok], paper1bc[3][ok], color = :gray)\nfor (x, T, lag, mort, name, c) in samples\n    scatterlines!(ax_a, binned(x, T, w), color = c, label = name)\n    pos = lag .> 0\n    scatterlines!(ax_b, binned(x[pos], lag[pos], w[pos]), color = c)\n    scatterlines!(ax_c, binned(lag[pos], mort[pos], w[pos]), color = c)\nend\npos = runs.lag .> 0\nscatter!(ax_d, runs.lag[pos], capT(runs.T)[pos], color = (:crimson, 0.4), markersize = 5)\nℓ = exp.(range(log(minimum(runs.lag[pos])), log(maximum(runs.lag[pos])), length = 100))\nfitp = logfit(t.lag, t.T)\nlines!(ax_d, ℓ, min.(exp.(fitp.intercept .+ fitp.slope .* log.(ℓ) .+ fitp.quadratic .* log.(ℓ) .^ 2), 2.4e5),\n       color = :gray, label = \"paper's fit\")\naxislegend(ax_a, position = :lb)\naxislegend(ax_d, position = :lb)\nfig\n\n<div id=\"fig-figure1\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 1: Figure 1 of the paper (gray, from its figure data) with the same summaries of the sampled populations: their published runs (navy) and their QTLSim reruns (crimson). (a) Median generations to extinction against the standardised rate of environmental change; medians above 2000 generations are drawn at 2000, the length of the reruns. (b) Median standardised lag. (c) Median mortality at generation 47 against the median lag. (d) Generations to extinction against standardised lag for the QTLSim reruns, with the paper’s fitted curve.\n\n</div>\n\nThe QTLSim reruns (crimson) and the published runs of the same populations (navy) lie on the paper’s curves (gray) in every panel. Populations facing change slower than about 0.07 phenotypic standard deviations per generation mostly outlast the 2000 generations of the reruns, so their median is drawn at the cap; beyond that the time to extinction falls steeply, to about a hundred generations at half a standard deviation per generation. The lag grows in proportion to the rate of change, and mortality at generation 47 rises from almost none at lags of a few hundredths of a standard deviation per generation to about half at a lag of 0.2 and nearly all at a lag of 1.\n\nThe quadratic fit of Figure 1d, made to the two samples with times capped at 2000:\n\nprintln(\"| fit | intercept | slope | quadratic | R² | populations |\\n|:--|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|\")\nfor (name, f) in ((\"paper (all populations, uncapped)\", logfit(t.lag, t.T)),\n                  (\"published runs of the sample, capped\", logfit(pub(:lag), capT(pub(:T)), w)),\n                  (\"QTLSim reruns of the sample, capped\", logfit(runs.lag, capT(runs.T), w)))\n    @printf(\"| %s | %.2f | %.3f | %.4f | %.2f | %d |\\n\", name, f.intercept, f.slope, f.quadratic, f.R2, f.n)\nend\n\nfit intercept slope quadratic R² populations\npaper (all populations, uncapped) 4.00 -0.913 -0.0183 0.83 119801\npublished runs of the sample, capped 4.06 -0.863 -0.0451 0.60 709\nQTLSim reruns of the sample, capped 4.04 -0.912 -0.0544 0.61 707\n\nCapped at 2000 generations, the published runs and the QTLSim reruns give almost the same fit. Both differ from the paper’s fit to uncapped times: the cap flattens the times of the longest-lived populations, which lowers R^2 to about 0.6."},{"title":"Genetic variance at equilibrium","page":"Replicating Pagel, Gardner and Meade (2025)","location":"tutorials/09_replicating_pagel_2025/#Genetic-variance-at-equilibrium","category":"section","text":"The paper checks its populations’ genetic variance at the end of the stabilising phase against Bürger’s stochastic house-of-cards approximation (Bürger et al. 1989) (its Supplementary Figure S1; see Mutation–selection balance). The same check for the reruns, with the effective population size computed from each rerun’s own number of survivors:\n\nfunction house_of_cards(i, ns)\n    N, n, μ = POP, t.L[i], 1e-4\n    VE = (1 - t.h2[i]) * t.VP[i]\n    Vs = t.ω[i]^2 + VE\n    α2 = t.m[i] * VE / (2n * μ)\n    Ne = 4N / (2N * (ns - 1) / ns^2 + 2)\n    4n * μ * Vs / (1 + Vs / (Ne * α2))\nend\nshc_jl = [house_of_cards(i, ns) for (i, ns) in zip(runs.row, runs.ns_eq)]\n@printf(\"median V_G / house of cards: published runs %.2f, QTLSim reruns %.2f\\n\",\n        wquantile(pub(:VG) ./ pub(:shc), w, 0.5), wquantile(runs.VG ./ shc_jl, w, 0.5))\n\nmedian V_G / house of cards: published runs 0.90, QTLSim reruns 0.89\n\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 400))\nfor (k, (pred, obs, name, c)) in enumerate(((pub(:shc), pub(:VG), \"published runs\", :navy),\n                                            (shc_jl, runs.VG, \"QTLSim reruns\", :crimson)))\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, k], xscale = log10, yscale = log10, title = name,\n              xlabel = \"house of cards\", ylabel = \"simulated V_G\")\n    ok = (pred .> 0) .& (obs .> 0)\n    lo, hi = extrema(vcat(pred[ok], obs[ok]))\n    lines!(ax, [lo, hi], [lo, hi], color = :gray, linestyle = :dash)\n    scatter!(ax, pred[ok], obs[ok], color = (c, 0.4), markersize = 5)\nend\nfig\n\n<div id=\"fig-shc\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 2: Genetic variance just before the optimum moves against the stochastic house-of-cards approximation, for the published runs (left) and the QTLSim reruns (right) of the sampled populations. The line is equality.\n\n</div>"},{"title":"How fast is too fast","page":"Replicating Pagel, Gardner and Meade (2025)","location":"tutorials/09_replicating_pagel_2025/#How-fast-is-too-fast","category":"section","text":"Which populations survive depends above all on the standardised rate of environmental change. The fraction still alive 2000 generations after the move, in bins of x, for all published populations (alive at 2000 generations, whatever happened later), for the published runs of the sample and for its QTLSim reruns:\n\nfig = Figure(size = (640, 400))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xscale = log10, xlabel = \"standardised rate of environmental change x\",\n          ylabel = \"alive after 2000 generations\")\nmean_in_bins(x, y, w; nbins) = begin\n    o = sortperm(x); c = cumsum(w[o]) ./ sum(w)\n    bin = min.(max.(ceil.(Int, c .* nbins), 1), nbins)\n    [(wquantile(x[o][bin .== b], w[o][bin .== b], 0.5),\n      sum(w[o][bin .== b] .* y[o][bin .== b]) / sum(w[o][bin .== b])) for b in 1:nbins if any(==(b), bin)]\nend\nscatterlines!(ax, mean_in_bins(t.x, Float64.(t.T .>= CAP), ones(length(t.x)); nbins = 50),\n              color = :gray, label = \"all published\")\nscatterlines!(ax, mean_in_bins(pub(:x), Float64.(pub(:T) .>= CAP), w; nbins = 24),\n              color = :navy, label = \"published sample\")\nscatterlines!(ax, mean_in_bins(runs.x, Float64.(.!runs.extinct), w; nbins = 24),\n              color = :crimson, label = \"QTLSim sample\")\naxislegend(ax, position = :rt)\nfig\n\n<div id=\"fig-survival\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 3: Fraction of populations still alive 2000 generations after the optimum starts moving, against the standardised rate of environmental change, for all 119,984 published populations (gray), the published runs of the sampled populations (navy) and their QTLSim reruns (crimson).\n\n</div>\n\nalive = .!runs.extinct; alive_pub = pub(:T) .>= CAP\n@printf(\"rate of phenotypic change of populations alive after 2000 generations: QTLSim %s, published runs %s\\n\",\n        iqr(runs.rate_ext[alive], w[alive]), iqr(pub(:rate_ext)[alive_pub], w[alive_pub]))\n\nrate of phenotypic change of populations alive after 2000 generations: QTLSim 0.032 (0.0177–0.0678), published runs 0.0363 (0.018–0.0847)\n\nIn all three, half the populations are still alive 2000 generations after the move when the environment moves by about 0.07 phenotypic standard deviations per generation, and almost none above about 0.2. The populations still alive have changed at a median of about 0.03 standard deviations per generation. The paper’s populations that lasted all 240,000 generations changed at a median of 0.031, the basis of its estimate of a maximum sustainable rate of about 3% of a standard deviation per generation."},{"title":"Survivors","page":"Replicating Pagel, Gardner and Meade (2025)","location":"tutorials/09_replicating_pagel_2025/#Survivors","category":"section","text":"In the paper, 6158 of the 119,984 populations (5.1%) were still alive 240,000 generations after their optimum started to move. Its maximum sustainable rate is the survivors’ median rate of phenotypic change, and its comparison with the field uses their critical rate. The sampled populations whose reruns were still alive after 2000 generations are run on to 240,000 generations, stored next to this vignette as the other runs are. Each is the same run carried further, so its measures before the move are those of its 2000-generation rerun:\n\nconst FULL = 240_000\nlong = run_replication(t, runs.row[.!runs.extinct], SURVIVOR_CACHE; cap = FULL)\nk = indexin(long.row, runs.row)\n@printf(\"%d reruns run on to %d generations; the same runs before the move: %s\\n\", length(long.row), FULL,\n        long.VG == runs.VG[k] && long.ns_eq == runs.ns_eq[k] && isequal(long.P47, runs.P47[k]))\n\n63 reruns run on to 240000 generations; the same runs before the move: true\n\nWhich of the sampled populations survived, in their published runs and in their reruns:\n\nsurvived_jl = falses(length(runs.row)); survived_jl[k] .= .!long.extinct\nsurvived_pub = pub(:survived)\nshare(alive, w = ones(length(alive))) = 100 * sum(w .* alive) / sum(w)\nprintln(\"| | rerun survived | rerun died |\\n|:--|--:|--:|\")\n@printf(\"| published run survived | %d | %d |\\n\", count(survived_pub .& survived_jl), count(survived_pub .& .!survived_jl))\n@printf(\"| published run died | %d | %d |\\n\", count(.!survived_pub .& survived_jl), count(.!survived_pub .& .!survived_jl))\n@printf(\"\\nSurvived 240,000 generations, strata weighted equally: QTLSim %.1f%%, published runs %.1f%%; all published populations %.1f%%.\\n\",\n        share(survived_jl, w), share(survived_pub, w), share(t.survived))\n\n rerun survived rerun died\npublished run survived 40 1\npublished run died 0 669\n\nSurvived 240,000 generations, strata weighted equally: QTLSim 5.4%, published runs 5.5%; all published populations 5.1%.\n\nThe survivors’ statistics, for the reruns measured as the table measures the published runs: the rate of phenotypic change over all 240,000 generations, and k_c from each rerun’s own genetic variance, survivors at generation 10,047 and phenotypic standard deviation at 50% mortality.\n\nsv = findall(.!long.extinct)                                  # surviving reruns\nrows_jl = long.row[sv]; w_jl = [1 / SAMPLE_SIZE[L] for L in long.L[sv]]\nrate_jl = long.rate_ext[sv]\nkc_jl = [critical_rate(n = long.P47[i], VG = long.VG[i], ω = t.ω[r], VE = (1 - t.h2[r]) * t.VP[r],\n                       sd50 = long.sd50[i]) for (i, r) in zip(sv, rows_jl)]\nrows_pub = runs.row[survived_pub]; w_pub = w[survived_pub]   # surviving published runs of the sample\nadj_pub = mutation_adjusted(t.rate_ext[rows_pub], t.m[rows_pub], w_pub)\nadj_jl = mutation_adjusted(rate_jl, t.m[rows_jl], w_jl)\nprintln(\"| survivors' statistic | paper | all published survivors | published runs of the sample | QTLSim reruns |\")\nprintln(\"|:--|:--|:--|:--|:--|\")\nfor (name, paper, all_pub, s_pub, s_jl) in (\n        (\"rate of phenotypic change\", \"0.031 (0.011–0.066)\", t.rate_ext[t.survived], t.rate_ext[rows_pub], rate_jl),\n        (\"the same, adjusted for the mutation scalar\", \"0.019 (0.006–0.055)\", adj, adj_pub, adj_jl),\n        (\"critical rate k_c\", \"0.022 (0.008–0.056)\", t.kc[t.survived], t.kc[rows_pub], kc_jl))\n    println(\"| \", name, \" | \", paper, \" | \", iqr(all_pub), \" | \", iqr(s_pub, w_pub), \" | \", iqr(s_jl, w_jl), \" |\")\nend\nboth = intersect(rows_pub, rows_jl); ib = indexin(both, rows_jl)\n@printf(\"\\nIn the %d populations that survived in both, the rerun's rate of phenotypic change is %.3f times the published one and its k_c %.3f times (medians of the ratios).\\n\",\n        length(both), median(rate_jl[ib] ./ t.rate_ext[both]), median(filter(isfinite, kc_jl[ib] ./ t.kc[both])))\n\nsurvivors’ statistic paper all published survivors published runs of the sample QTLSim reruns\nrate of phenotypic change 0.031 (0.011–0.066) 0.0307 (0.0117–0.0661) 0.0375 (0.0193–0.0741) 0.0392 (0.0182–0.0735)\nthe same, adjusted for the mutation scalar 0.019 (0.006–0.055) 0.0197 (0.00656–0.0551) 0.0158 (0.011–0.0359) 0.0162 (0.0118–0.0344)\ncritical rate k_c 0.022 (0.008–0.056) 0.0222 (0.00821–0.0559) 0.0312 (0.00986–0.0612) 0.0303 (0.0112–0.0631)\n\nIn the 40 populations that survived in both, the rerun’s rate of phenotypic change is 0.994 times the published one and its k_c 1.017 times (medians of the ratios).\n\nThe reruns survive in the same populations as the published runs, and their survivors change at the same rates and have the same critical rates, population by population. The survivors of the sample have higher medians than all the paper’s survivors, in the published runs as much as in the reruns: with about forty survivors that difference is sampling error in the choice of populations, not a difference between QTSim and QTLSim."},{"title":"Summary","page":"Replicating Pagel, Gardner and Meade (2025)","location":"tutorials/09_replicating_pagel_2025/#Summary","category":"section","text":"The paper’s numbers next to the same quantities recomputed from its table, from the published runs of the 710 sampled populations, and from their QTLSim reruns (medians; for the samples, times capped at 2000 generations and strata weighted equally):\n\nmed(v, w = ones(length(v))) = (ok = isfinite.(v); wquantile(v[ok], w[ok], 0.5))\nft, fp, fj = logfit(t.lag, t.T), logfit(pub(:lag), capT(pub(:T)), w), logfit(runs.lag, capT(runs.T), w)\nprintln(\"| result | paper | paper's table | published runs of the sample | QTLSim reruns |\")\nprintln(\"|:--|:--|:--|:--|:--|\")\n@printf(\"| generations to extinction | 141 | %.0f | %.0f | %.0f |\\n\",\n        med(t.T), med(capT(pub(:T)), w), med(capT(runs.T), w))\n@printf(\"| generations to 50%% mortality | 47 | %.0f | %.0f | %.0f |\\n\",\n        med(t.T50), med(pub(:T50), w), med(Float64.(runs.T50), w))\n@printf(\"| rate of phenotypic change to extinction | 0.056 | %.3f | %.3f | %.3f |\\n\",\n        med(t.rate_ext), med(pub(:rate_ext), w), med(runs.rate_ext, w))\n@printf(\"| rate of phenotypic change to 50%% mortality | 0.033 | %.3f | %.3f | %.3f |\\n\",\n        med(t.rate50), med(pub(:rate50), w), med(runs.rate50, w))\n@printf(\"| slope of log time to extinction on log lag | −0.91 | %.2f | %.2f (capped) | %.2f (capped) |\\n\",\n        ft.slope, fp.slope, fj.slope)\n@printf(\"| genetic variance / house of cards | close to 1 | | %.2f | %.2f |\\n\",\n        med(pub(:VG) ./ pub(:shc), w), med(runs.VG ./ shc_jl, w))\n@printf(\"| alive 2000 generations after the move | | %.1f%% | %.1f%% | %.1f%% |\\n\",\n        share(t.T .>= CAP), share(pub(:T) .>= CAP, w), share(.!runs.extinct, w))\n@printf(\"| survived 240,000 generations | 5.1%% | %.1f%% | %.1f%% | %.1f%% |\\n\",\n        share(t.survived), share(survived_pub, w), share(survived_jl, w))\n@printf(\"| rate of phenotypic change, survivors | 0.031 | %.3f | %.3f | %.3f |\\n\",\n        med(t.rate_ext[t.survived]), med(t.rate_ext[rows_pub], w_pub), med(rate_jl, w_jl))\n@printf(\"| the same, adjusted for the mutation scalar | 0.019 | %.3f | %.3f | %.3f |\\n\",\n        med(adj), med(adj_pub, w_pub), med(adj_jl, w_jl))\n@printf(\"| critical rate k_c, survivors | 0.022 | %.3f | %.3f | %.3f |\\n\",\n        med(t.kc[t.survived]), med(t.kc[rows_pub], w_pub), med(kc_jl, w_jl))\n@printf(\"| percentiles of k_c at 0.044 and 0.088 | 68th, 86th | %.0fth, %.0fth | | |\\n\",\n        100 * mean(kc .<= 0.044), 100 * mean(kc .<= 0.088))\n\nresult paper paper’s table published runs of the sample QTLSim reruns\ngenerations to extinction 141 141 138 137\ngenerations to 50% mortality 47 47 45 45\nrate of phenotypic change to extinction 0.056 0.056 0.056 0.058\nrate of phenotypic change to 50% mortality 0.033 0.033 0.033 0.033\nslope of log time to extinction on log lag −0.91 -0.91 -0.86 (capped) -0.91 (capped)\ngenetic variance / house of cards close to 1  0.90 0.89\nalive 2000 generations after the move  8.1% 9.0% 8.8%\nsurvived 240,000 generations 5.1% 5.1% 5.5% 5.4%\nrate of phenotypic change, survivors 0.031 0.031 0.038 0.039\nthe same, adjusted for the mutation scalar 0.019 0.020 0.016 0.016\ncritical rate k_c, survivors 0.022 0.022 0.031 0.030\npercentiles of k_c at 0.044 and 0.088 68th, 86th 68th, 86th  \n\nEvery number the paper reports that can be computed from its results table is reproduced exactly from it, including the adjustment for the mutation scalar and the critical rate k_c, whose recipes are recovered here. On a stratified sample of 710 of its populations, QTLSim reproduces the published runs: survival, rates of change, lag, mortality and genetic variance agree with the published runs of the same populations to within the scatter between two realisations of each population, and with the paper’s figures. Run on to the paper’s horizon of 240,000 generations, the reruns survive in the same populations as the published runs, and their survivors’ rates of change and critical rates agree with the published ones to within a few per cent.\n\n<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-BurgerLynch1995\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nBürger, Reinhard, and Michael Lynch. 1995. “Evolution and Extinction in a Changing Environment: A Quantitative-Genetic Analysis.” Evolution 49 (1): 151–63. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1558-5646.1995.tb05967.x.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Burger1989\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nBürger, Reinhard, Günter P. Wagner, and Franz Stettinger. 1989. “How Much Heritable Variation Can Be Maintained in Finite Populations by Mutation-Selection Balance?” Evolution 43 (8): 1748–66. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1558-5646.1989.tb02624.x.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-LynchLande1993\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nLynch, Michael, and Russell Lande. 1993. “Evolution and Extinction in Response to Environmental Change.” In Biotic Interactions and Global Change, edited by Peter M. Kareiva, Joel G. Kingsolver, and Raymond B. Huey. Sinauer.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Pagel2025\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nPagel, Mark, Jacob D. Gardner, and Andrew Meade. 2025. “Quantitative-Genetic Analysis of Directional Adaptation Suggests Low Maximum Sustainable Rates of Change in Agreement with Data from Field Populations.” Scientific Reports 15. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-24445-2.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"title":"Getting started","page":"Getting started","location":"tutorials/01_getting_started/#Getting-started","category":"section","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nSetup\nParameters\nA first run\nReproducibility\nLetting the optimum move\nWhere next"},{"title":"Overview","page":"Getting started","location":"tutorials/01_getting_started/#Overview","category":"section","text":"QTLSim.jl simulates the quantitative-genetic model of Pagel, Gardner and Meade (Pagel et al. 2025), who used it to ask how fast a population can track a steadily changing environment before it goes extinct. Their simulations were run with QTSim, a C program by Andrew Meade; this package is a Julia port of it, built around a fast kernel that reproduces QTSim’s output (see Validation against QTSim). An optional categorical layer, loaded with Catlab, describes the population and its life cycle as a composition of operators (The categorical layer).\n\nThe model follows a population of N diploid individuals with one quantitative trait.\n\nEach individual carries two copies of NoQTL loci. Its genotype G is the sum of the allelic effects at all of them, and its phenotype is z = G + E, where the environmental deviation E is drawn from mathcalN(0 V_E) at birth.\nSelection is on viability: an individual survives with probability W(z) = expleft(-(z-theta)^2  2omega^2right), a Gaussian fitness function with optimum theta and width omega (up to a constant close to one, which the package keeps exactly as QTSim computes it).\nThe survivors mate at random. Each of the N offspring has two distinct parents, each of which passes on a gamete made by recombining its two chromosomes.\nEach offspring chromosome receives a Poisson number of mutations, each adding a Gaussian effect to a randomly chosen locus.\n\nThis vignette runs the model once, looks at what it returns, and then lets the optimum move, which is the experiment at the heart of the paper."},{"title":"Setup","page":"Getting started","location":"tutorials/01_getting_started/#Setup","category":"section","text":"using QTLSim\nusing CairoMakie, Printf, Statistics\nCairoMakie.activate!(type = \"png\")"},{"title":"Parameters","page":"Getting started","location":"tutorials/01_getting_started/#Parameters","category":"section","text":"A simulation is described by a Params value. Its field names and defaults follow the options of QTSim’s input files, so a published input file translates field by field. The phenotypic variance V_P and heritability h^2 fix the genetic and environmental variances the model aims for, V_G = h^2 V_P and V_E = (1-h^2) V_P; the mutation scalar m sets the new variance mutation adds each generation, V_m = m V_E.\n\np = Params(PopSize = 1000, Ploidy = 2, NoQTL = 100, NoGenerations = 3000,\n           PhenotypeVar = 0.02, Heritability = 0.4, MutationScalar = 0.004,\n           MutationRatePerGamete = 1e-4, RecombPoissonMean = 3.0)\n\n@printf(\"target genetic variance V_G = h²·V_P      %.4f\\n\", geneticVar(p))\n@printf(\"environmental variance  V_E = (1−h²)·V_P  %.4f\\n\", envVar(p))\n@printf(\"mutational input        V_m = m·V_E       %.2e per generation\\n\", muteVar(p))\n@printf(\"fitness width           ω                 %.3f\\n\", initFitSD(p))\n\ntarget genetic variance V_G = h²·V_P      0.0080\nenvironmental variance  V_E = (1−h²)·V_P  0.0120\nmutational input        V_m = m·V_E       4.80e-05 per generation\nfitness width           ω                 1.155\n\nMutationRatePerGamete is the mutation rate per locus per gamete, so each chromosome receives on average NoQTL × MutationRatePerGamete = 0.01 mutations per generation. RecombPoissonMean is the mean number of crossover points drawn per gamete. When InitFitnessSD is left at its default, the width omega is QTSim’s “analytical” value omega = V_G  sqrtm V_E; the next vignette explains where it comes from."},{"title":"A first run","page":"Getting started","location":"tutorials/01_getting_started/#A-first-run","category":"section","text":"simulate builds the population, runs it for NoGenerations generations and returns one record per sampled generation (every generation here, since Sample = 1). The genomes start at zero, so the genetic variance starts at zero too and builds up through mutation.\n\nout = simulate(p; seed = 1)\nkeys(out)\n\n(:extinct, :extinct_gen, :gen, :pheno_mean, :pheno_var, :geno_var, :nsurv, :theta, :omega)\n\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 300))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"generation\", ylabel = \"mean phenotype\")\nlines!(ax1, out.gen, out.pheno_mean, color = :navy)\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], xlabel = \"generation\", ylabel = \"genetic variance\")\nlines!(ax2, out.gen, out.geno_var, color = :teal)\nhlines!(ax2, [geneticVar(p)], color = :gray, linestyle = :dash)\nax3 = Axis(fig[1, 3], xlabel = \"generation\", ylabel = \"survivors\")\nlines!(ax3, out.gen, out.nsurv, color = :crimson)\nfig\n\n<div id=\"fig-first-run\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 1: One population under stabilising selection. The mean phenotype stays near the optimum (zero), the genetic variance builds up from zero to a balance between mutation, selection and drift, and almost every individual survives selection each generation.\n\n</div>\n\nThe dashed line is the target h^2 V_P. The variance the population actually maintains is set by the balance of mutation, selection and drift, not by the target; the next vignette compares it with theory.\n\nlate = out.gen .>= 2000\n@printf(\"mean genetic variance over generations 2000–2999: %.4f\\n\", mean(out.geno_var[late]))\n@printf(\"mean number of survivors:                          %.1f of %d\\n\",\n        mean(out.nsurv[late]), p.PopSize)\n\nmean genetic variance over generations 2000–2999: 0.0270\nmean number of survivors:                          982.8 of 1000"},{"title":"Reproducibility","page":"Getting started","location":"tutorials/01_getting_started/#Reproducibility","category":"section","text":"Runs are driven by a seeded random-number generator, so the same seed gives the same run, and a different seed gives a different realisation of the same process.\n\nsame = simulate(p; seed = 1)\nother = simulate(p; seed = 2)\n(same_seed_identical = same.geno_var == out.geno_var,\n other_seed_identical = other.geno_var == out.geno_var)\n\n(same_seed_identical = true, other_seed_identical = false)"},{"title":"Letting the optimum move","page":"Getting started","location":"tutorials/01_getting_started/#Letting-the-optimum-move","category":"section","text":"In the paper’s experiments the optimum stands still while the population reaches equilibrium, then moves by a fixed amount d every generation (MoveType = :continuous, starting at MoveFitnessSDGen). A population that can keep up follows the optimum with a constant lag; one that cannot falls further and further behind, its survival collapses, and it goes extinct. Here the same population faces a slow and a fast rate of change, starting at generation 2000. Rates are easiest to compare in units of the phenotypic standard deviation, which we measure just before the move.\n\nmoving(d) = Params(p; NoGenerations = 3000, MoveFitnessSDGen = 2000,\n                   MoveFitnessSD = d, MoveType = :continuous)\nslow = simulate(moving(0.002); seed = 1)\nfast = simulate(moving(0.05); seed = 1)\nsdP = sqrt(mean(slow.pheno_var[1900 .<= slow.gen .< 2000]))\n@printf(\"phenotypic SD before the move: %.3f\\n\", sdP)\n@printf(\"slow: d = 0.002 is %4.1f%% of an SD per generation; extinct: %s\\n\",\n        100 * 0.002 / sdP, slow.extinct)\n@printf(\"fast: d = 0.05  is %4.1f%% of an SD per generation; extinct at generation %d\\n\",\n        100 * 0.05 / sdP, fast.extinct_gen)\n\nphenotypic SD before the move: 0.188\nslow: d = 0.002 is  1.1% of an SD per generation; extinct: false\nfast: d = 0.05  is 26.6% of an SD per generation; extinct at generation 2184\n\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 330))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"generation\", ylabel = \"phenotype / optimum\",\n           limits = ((1800, 3000), nothing))\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], xlabel = \"generation\", ylabel = \"survivors\",\n           limits = ((1800, 3000), (0, 1050)))\nfor (r, c, name) in ((slow, :teal, \"d = 0.002\"), (fast, :crimson, \"d = 0.05\"))\n    lines!(ax1, r.gen, r.pheno_mean, color = c, label = name)\n    lines!(ax1, r.gen, r.theta, color = c, linestyle = :dash)\n    lines!(ax2, r.gen, r.nsurv, color = c, label = name)\nend\naxislegend(ax1, position = :lt)\nfig\n\n<div id=\"fig-moving\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 2: The optimum (dashed) starts moving at generation 2000. At the slow rate the mean phenotype (solid) keeps pace and survival stays high; at the fast rate the population falls further and further behind, survival collapses and the population dies out.\n\n</div>\n\nThis population can keep up with surprisingly fast change: it holds a lot of genetic variance, and its narrow fitness function turns a small lag into strong selection. Across the range of populations the paper simulated, sustainable change is limited to a few per cent of a standard deviation per generation; Adaptation to a moving optimum measures this the way the paper does, and Replicating Pagel, Gardner and Meade (2025) reproduces the paper’s results."},{"title":"Where next","page":"Getting started","location":"tutorials/01_getting_started/#Where-next","category":"section","text":"Mutation–selection balance: how much genetic variance the population maintains, compared with theory.\nAdaptation to a moving optimum: the ways the optimum can move, and the paper’s measures of lag and extinction.\nThe categorical layer and Recomposing the model: the population as an ACSet, and a generation as a composite of operators that can be rearranged.\nLoci and recombination: many loci, and the way QTSim recombines them.\nValidation against QTSim and Replicating Pagel, Gardner and Meade (2025).\n\n<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-Pagel2025\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nPagel, Mark, Jacob D. Gardner, and Andrew Meade. 2025. “Quantitative-Genetic Analysis of Directional Adaptation Suggests Low Maximum Sustainable Rates of Change in Agreement with Data from Field Populations.” Scientific Reports 15. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-24445-2.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>"}]
}
